\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:method}
% Fig.~\ref{fig:overview} presents a high-level overview of our lightweight profiling framework, which evaluates the end-to-end performance and energy cost of serving transformer-based language models on a range of edge platforms. The framework is implemented in Python and is designed to decouple model characteristics, hardware capabilities, and numeric precision. This modular approach allows for rapid “what-if” analysis to explore trade-offs across devices, precisions, and model sizes, without requiring full deployment or measurement on each target system.

Fig.~\ref{fig:overview} presents a high-level overview of our lightweight profiling framework, which evaluates the end-to-end performance and energy cost of serving transformer-based language models on a range of edge platforms. The framework operates through a modular workflow: it takes as input the model configuration, hardware specifications, and numeric precision settings; internally, it performs analytical modeling of key performance metrics such as parameter count, FLOPs, memory footprint, latency, and energy consumption; and finally, it outputs detailed performance estimates, enabling trade-off exploration across different device-model-precision combinations. This setup provides a scalable method for understanding and comparing LLM behavior on resource-constrained systems without requiring full hardware deployment.

\subsection{Performance Modeling Framework}
\noindent
At the core of our framework is the EdgeProfile, which takes three inputs: model configuration, hardware configuration, and precision configuration.

\minisection{Model configuration.} Specifies transformer architecture parameters: number of layers ($L$), hidden dimension ($H$), intermediate dimension ($I$), attention heads, vocabulary size ($V$), and sequence length ($S$).

\minisection{Hardware configuration.} Encapsulates peak computational throughput (FLOPs/sec), DRAM bandwidth, storage throughput, PCIe host‐to‐device bandwidth, interconnect/network bandwidth, utilization factors ($U_\text{compute}$, $U_\text{memory}$, $U_\text{storage}$, $U_\text{H2D}$, $U_\text{net}$), and energy cost per flop or per byte accessed.

\minisection{Precision configuration.} Defines the data‐type size $B$ in bytes (4B for FP32, 2B for FP16, 1B for INT8).


\noindent
\minisection{EdgeProfiler computes}:

\paragraph{Parameter count}
\begin{equation}
\label{weight_embeddings}
  P = L\,(4H^2) + L\,(2HI) + 2VH
\end{equation}
Equation \ref{weight_embeddings} captures all of the model’s weight parameters. The four projection matrices per layer plus the two feed-forward matrices, and the input/output embeddings.

\paragraph{FLOPs per token}
\begin{equation}
\label{flop}
  \begin{aligned}
    \mathrm{FLOPs/token}
    &= L\bigl(6H^2 + 4HS + 4HI + 4IH + 9H\bigr)
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Equation \ref{flop} accounts for both attention projections query, key, value, and output projection(Q,K,V,O) and the two matmuls in each feed‐forward block, as well as LayerNorm and Softmax overhead.

\paragraph{Memory footprint}
\begin{equation}
\label{memory}
  M = P\cdot B + S\cdot H\cdot B + 2L\cdot S\cdot H\cdot B
\end{equation}
Equation \ref{memory} includes storing all weights (\(P\cdot B\)), the hidden‐state activations for the full sequence (\(S\!H\!B\)), and the cached key/value pairs across layers (\(2L\!S\!H\!B\)).

\subsection{Latency Breakdown}
\noindent
Using the counts above, we estimate each component of on-chip inference time.

\paragraph{Compute latency}
\begin{equation}
\label{comp}
  T_{\mathrm{comp}} =
  \frac{\mathrm{FLOPs/token}}{\mathrm{peak}_{\mathrm{flops}} \times U_{\mathrm{compute}}}
\end{equation}
Equation \ref{comp} performs all the arithmetic at the device’s effective compute throughput.

\paragraph{Memory‐bound latency}
\begin{equation}
\label{mem}
  T_{\mathrm{mem}} =
  \frac{M}{\mathrm{memory}_{\mathrm{BW}} \times U_{\mathrm{memory}}}
\end{equation}
Equation \ref{mem} captures the delay reading/writing weights, activations, and cache from on-chip memory.

\paragraph{I/O, Host‐to‐Device, and Network Overheads}
Each is computed by dividing the total weight bytes ($P\cdot B$) or shard bytes by the respective bandwidth and utilization factor:
\begin{equation}
\label{I/O}
  T_{\mathrm{I/O}}   = \frac{P\,B}{\mathrm{STORAGE}_{\mathrm{BW}} \times U_{\mathrm{storage}}}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\label{h2d}
  T_{\mathrm{h2d}}  = \frac{P\,B}{\mathrm{H2D}_{\mathrm{BW}} \times U_{\mathrm{H2D}}}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\label{net}
  T_{\mathrm{net}}  = \frac{S\,H\,B}{\mathrm{NET}_{\mathrm{BW}} \times U_{\mathrm{net}}}
\end{equation}
Equation \ref{I/O} is the latency to load model weights from disk or flash storage. Equation \ref{h2d} is the latency to copy weights over PCIe or NVLink into GPU memory.
Equation \ref{net} is the latency to exchange a shard of key/value cache across nodes.
We also provide a fine‐grained breakdown of attention‐projection, key/value matmuls, MLP, layernorm, and softmax by dividing each operator’s raw FLOP count by effective compute throughput.

\subsection{Energy Modeling}
\noindent
Energy per token is estimated as the sum of compute and data‐movement costs:
\begin{equation}
  E = \mathrm{FLOPs/token}\,\times\,e_{\mathrm{flop}}
    \;+\; M \,\times\,e_{\mathrm{byte}},
\end{equation}
where \(e_{\mathrm{flop}}\) and \(e_{\mathrm{byte}}\) are measured in joules per FLOP and per byte, respectively.

This comprehensive modeling approach allows for systematic exploration of design trade-offs, helping identify bottlenecks and optimize configurations for efficient LLM deployment on edge systems.

