\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

% Continued advancements in LLM deployment on edge devices must address the core challenges of memory efficiency, computational cost, and energy constraints. Future research should explore the development of adaptive models that integrate low-bit quantization, hardware-aware training, and dynamic weight loading to optimize inference efficiency. Further, the convergence of TinyML frameworks with LLM architectures could unlock new capabilities for the deployment of multimodal models, such as vision-language systems, on constrained platforms. 
In this paper we have propose EdgeProfiler, a fast profiling framework for lightweight LLMs on edge devices using an analytical model. EdgeProfiler framework reveals how hardware characteristics, model size, and numerical precision jointly shape inference performance and efficiency. We present our findings from three widely used edge devices.
Across all platforms, reducing precision from FP32, FP16, and INT8 yields substantial end-to-end latency and energy savings. INT8 cuts the latency by $\sim$75\% and energy by  $\sim$75\% relative to FP32. On low-power devices, Raspberry Pi, memory, and I/O bound stage often dominate, whereas on more capable hardware like Jetson Nano, compute latency becomes negligible, and I/O or network transfer surface as the new bottleneck. Building upon this foundation, future work will focus on refining profiling granularity, integrating real-world deployment feedback, and expanding support for emerging hardware platforms to accelerate practical, scalable, and energy-efficient LLM applications on the edge.

% Large Language Models (LLMs) have been primarily utilized in cloud-based applications but are expanding to also apply to edge device deployment. These applications include research areas ranging from agriculture, language translation, medical applications, mobile devices, and emergency response situations. Despite the extreme memory, computation, and power requirements already involved in LLMs that are used on cloud or servers, the research into this type of deep learning model has improved significantly to the point of edge device integration. Strategies with matrix multiplication, pruning, quantization and compression allow for the LLM constraints to not limit the variety of applications that LLMs can be applied in when it comes to low-power and low-memory devices.

