\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} advent of sixth-generation (6G) wireless networks is expected to significantly enhance Internet of Things (IoT) connectivity by leveraging the abundant spectrum in mmWave or sub-THz bands \cite{6GIoT_survey}. In particular, industrial IoT (IIoT) is positioned as a foundational technology to enable intelligent industrial automation, where diverse connected devices collaborate to provide real-time visibility throughout the supply chain and monitoring under strict reliability and latency requirements \cite{smartmanufacturing, manufacturing}. Leading global platforms, such as Amazon Web Services (AWS) and Microsoft Azure, have already deployed IIoT platforms designed to manage large-scale industrial assets and services \cite{Huang2018Globalcompany,aws_2021_aws, gondosubroto2024aws_industry, Azure}. In general, IIoT devices are commonly categorized into stationary devices, such as manufacturing robots for precision production tasks, and mobile devices, such as automated guided vehicles (AGVs) and logistics robots for material handling \cite{AGV_IIoT}. Among various approaches to support heterogeneous IIoT devices, conventional network-based positioning algorithms can suffer from performance degradation due to multipath and interference in indoor smart factories with numerous metal-clad devices \cite{xu2024isac_vs_conv, wang2025isac_vs_conv}. By contrast, integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) systems provide a unified framework that employs radar sensing techniques, enabling not only accurate localization but also mobility and direction estimation by leveraging the information of echo channels explicitly modeled for sensing targets, which is essential for future IIoT systems \cite{IIoT_ISAC1, IIoT_ISAC2}.
