Recent studies have intensively investigated reconfigurable intelligent‑surface (RIS)‑aided ISAC systems to achieve further performance enhancements by adjusting the amplitude and phase shift of each element and reconfiguring the wireless propagation environment \cite{Ruizhang_ISAC, Cho2024RIS, Yoo2024ISAC, NF_RIS, near_ris_isac, NF_ISAC,  ris_near_far_coexistence}. In \cite{Ruizhang_ISAC}, a RIS‑enabled ISAC system is presented to achieve the enhanced sensing accuracy for multiple users in the far-field communications with the planar wave channel model. However, for the upcoming 6G networks with the use of a massive number of antennas and the dramatically increased carrier frequency in mmWave and THz bands, the near-field region cannot be neglected as in the past generation. According to the definition of Rayleigh distance, also called the Fraunhofer distance \cite{Selvan2017Fraunhofer}, in the multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) scenario with the $0.36$ m antenna aperture at $28$ GHz, the near-field range becomes $100$ m. In addition, when the RIS is employed between the access point (AP) and user equipment (UE) or sensing target, the inter-node distance decreases, and the near-field propagation with the spherical wave needs to be considered for the future 6G, which becomes indispensable in RIS-aided ISAC systems. To this end, \cite{NF_RIS} extends the channel modeling framework by introducing a cascaded near-field model for RIS-assisted links, and \cite{near_ris_isac} demonstrates enhanced estimation accuracy when the RIS is placed in close proximity to both users and targets. Although near-field models impose stringent computational and estimation requirements, they can theoretically yield superior sensing and communication performances than counterparts that only consider far-field channels \cite{NF_ISAC}. Moreover, the recent works \cite{ris_near_far_coexistence, NF_FF_coexistence} have initiated the study of hybrid near-field and far‑field coexistence channel models, where the near‑field beam-focusing enhances estimation accuracy for close-range targets, while the far‑field plane‑wave model is employed beyond the focal range by effectively serving multiple users within a cone-shaped beam. Accordingly, the coexistence of near-field and far-field communications becomes an essential component for the design of RIS‑aided ISAC systems, which requires revisiting the conventional system design to accommodate both field conditions.


