Meanwhile, within the ISAC framework, since the sensing echoes and communication waveforms are concurrently received over the same radio frequency band in near-field or far-field ISAC scenarios, an appropriate multiple‑access mechanism becomes essential to ensure reliable decoding. Non‑orthogonal multiple access (NOMA) has emerged as a promising solution, as it enables the superposition of multiple signals within a single frequency band and facilitates their recovery via successive interference cancellation (SIC) \cite{NOMA, Wang2022noma_isac, Xue2025noma_isac2, noma_ris_isac_1, noma_ris_isac_2, Semi_ISAC, IIoT_noma}. The authors in \cite{Wang2022noma_isac, Xue2025noma_isac2} introduce an adaptive NOMA empowered ISAC framework that dynamically manages sensing and communication interference to further save transmit power consumption. In particular,\cite{noma_ris_isac_1, noma_ris_isac_2} demonstrate that the cluster‑based NOMA within RIS‑aided ISAC systems effectively improves resource utilization. Moreover,\cite{Semi_ISAC} offers a rigorous analytical framework to evaluate sensing accuracy under channel conditions, thereby providing practical guidelines for the deployment and optimization of NOMA-assisted ISAC systems.



