In this paper, we propose a RIS-assisted ISAC system as illustrated in Fig. \ref{System model} for future IIoT applications. 
%The AP receives two types of signals depending on the category of IIoT devices it serves. Specifically, the AP receives ISAC signals with superimposed sensing and uplink signals from IIoT devices such as mobile robots, while receiving communication signals assisted by RIS from IIoT devices such as manufacturing robots. Here we address both the sensing-only (SO) band and the integrated sensing and communications (ISAC) band. Using the NOMA technique, the AP sequentially decodes signals received in these frequency bands.
With the emerging coexistence of near-field and far-field communications, we newly address the RIS-assisted ISAC systems for 6G IIoT by introducing cooperative bandwidth splitting strategies, thereby contributing to the realization of Industry 6.0-driven intelligent manufacturing and future IIoT systems. The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows.
\begin{itemize}
%%%

    \item \textbf{Comprehensive survey on ISAC:} We classify and analyze state-of-the-art ISAC studies into four technical tracks, such as ISAC for IIoT, near-field ISAC and near-field and far-field coexistence, ISAC empowered by NOMA and RIS, and cooperative bandwidth splitting ISAC, highlighting key frameworks and design considerations for practical and scalable ISAC in 6G IIoT scenarios.
    \item \textbf{ISAC-based IIoT framework:} We consider an IIoT environment composed of a full-duplex AP, a RIS with passive reflecting elements and multiple types of IIoT devices. To take into account the coexistence of near-field and far-field devices arising in IIoT scenario, we consider both spherical and planar wave-based channel models according to the Fresnel region \cite{Selvan2017Fraunhofer}. Moreover, for the spectral efficiency, we explore a cooperative bandwidth splitting method that allocates an ISAC band alongside the traditional sensing-only (SO) band with the NOMA technique, enabling the prioritized decoding of communication signals from multiple IIoT devices, followed by the subsequent processing of sensing signals.
    % far field 와 near field의 coexistence 설명 추가 채널모델 spherical wave and planar wave
    %We adopt a spherical wave propagation model for near-field region and a planar wave propagation model for far-field region channel links 

    \item \textbf{RIS-enabled ISAC optimization:} To maximize the sensing accuracy of the proposed ISAC system in terms of Cram${\Grave{\textrm{e}}}$r-Rao bound (CRB), we formulate the problem to jointly optimize the RIS phase shifts, bandwidth splitting ratio and beamforming vector under the minimum individual data rate constraints of the IIoT device and resource budget requirements. To this end, we develop an algorithmic solution via the successive convex approximation (SCA)-based alternating optimization (AO) algorithm with semi-definite relaxation (SDR) technique, whose convergence and complexity analyses are theoretically and numerically provided.
    \item \textbf{IIoT standard-compatible simulations:} To validate the performance of the proposed system, we adopt parameter settings aligned with the 3GPP standards \cite{3gpp} that are widely used by global IIoT companies \cite{Huang2018Globalcompany,aws_2021_aws, gondosubroto2024aws_industry, Azure}. Via simulations, the proposed method is verified to achieve superior ISAC performance compared to conventional approaches, e.g., exploiting solely on either the ISAC or SO band, or employing fixed and equal bandwidth allocation, under various near-field and far-field configurations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Organization}
The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section \ref{System_Model} provides a comprehensive survey on the recent ISAC systems and introduces the set-up for the proposed RIS-assisted ISAC framework in IIoT scenarios and Section \ref{Communications and sensing protocols} introduces the communications and sensing protocols. Section \ref{Problem Formulation and Proposed Algorithm} formulates the CRB minimization problem and presents the proposed algorithmic solution with its convergence and complexity analyses. Section \ref{Numerical Results} provides numerical evaluations to verify the effectiveness of the proposed method. Finally, Section \ref{Conclusion} summarizes the paper and points to directions for future work.

