Leading global corporations, e.g., such as AWS \cite{aws_2021_aws, gondosubroto2024aws_industry} and Microsoft’s Azure IIoT \cite{Azure}, have already deployed IIoT systems and systematically assessed their performance and cost-effectiveness under realistic industrial scenarios \cite{Huang2018Globalcompany,aws_2021_aws, gondosubroto2024aws_industry,Azure}.
Most IIoT scenarios demand the integration of reliable connectivity and real-time situational awareness. Broader surveys \cite{IIoT_ISAC1, IIoT_ISAC2} highlights the practical utility of ISAC in smart factories, emphasizing its potential to reduce signaling overhead and enabling precise coordination among industrial robots and autonomous vehicles. The related measurement campaign is presented in \cite{IIoT_ISAC3}, emulating a dynamic industrial environment to characterize and analyze sub‑terahertz propagation features pertinent to ISAC applications. To overcome spectrum scarcity in dense IIoT environments, \cite{IIoT_noma} proposes a cluster‑head‑assisted NOMA scheme for cognitive IIoT networks, where a joint optimization of sensing time, node transmit power, and cluster count, outperforming conventional orthogonal multiple access (OMA)/NOMA baselines.



\subsubsection{Near‑Field ISAC and Near-field and Far‑Field Coexistence}
%Large‑aperture arrays shrink the Fresnel distance, making near‑field modelling indispensable. A semi‑passive RIS placed close to both user and target is analyzed in \cite{near_ris_isac}, where closed‑form surrogate CRBs for joint range‑and‑angle estimation are minimized via alternating optimization under minimum‑rate constraints. Channel‑model mismatches are tackled in \cite{ris_near_far_coexistence}, which blends Fresnel and Fraunhofer regions into a piece‑wise near‑field model and jointly optimizes active/passive beamforming to maximize spectral efficiency in the presence of estimation errors. To harmonize Fresnel and far‑field services, \cite{NF_FF_coexistence} re‑uses pre‑steered beams for near‑field users and overlays far‑field links via NOMA, revealing favourable massive‑MIMO scaling laws. More recently, a series of purely near‑field frameworks has emerged. In \cite{Li2024NF_FF}, the base station adopts a dual‑array architecture—a large main transceiver modeled with spherical waves and a smaller assisting array approximated by plane waves. Joint downlink/uplink optimization minimizes the angle \& distance CRB while maintaining user‑rate guarantees, and a hybrid analog–digital solution approaches fully‑digital positioning accuracy. Building upon the added radial degree of freedom, \cite{NF_ISAC} derives coupled distance \& angle CRBs and shows tangible sensing‑and‑rate gains over far‑field ISAC under per‑user rate constraints. Finally, \cite{nf_isac3} revisits ISAC when near‑field propagation meets ultra‑wideband signaling; it uncovers synergies that enable signal multiplexing and high‑resolution ranging, and outlines paradigm shifts in spectrum allocation, array geometry, and waveform design for future wideband near‑field ISAC systems.

Early near‑field ISAC studies \cite{NF_ISAC, Li2024NF_FF, nf_isac3} model the spherical wavefront within the Fresnel region of large antenna arrays and evaluate their frameworks in single-cell scenarios with line-of-sight users and static targets. 
%By leveraging distance‑aware beam focusing, these studies demonstrate significant gains in joint sensing‑and‑communication performance over conventional far‑field baselines, albeit with increased channel‑estimation and beamforming complexity when users transition beyond the Fresnel zone.
Building upon prior near-field ISAC works, \cite{near_ris_isac} inserts a semi‑passive RIS positioned near the user and target within the indoor factory‑hall environment, effectively enhancing link reliability and location estimation precision. Accordingly, the near-field ISAC provides enhanced resolution and beam-focusing, whereas the practical scenarios with varied link distances necessitate hybrid frameworks that accommodate near-field and far-field coexistence. The authors in \cite{NF_FF_coexistence} propose a NOMA-based massive MIMO system that serves far-field users via NOMA using pre-configured near-field spatial beams. Moreover, \cite{ris_near_far_coexistence} employs a piecewise near‑field and far‑field channel model for the RIS‑aided MIMO links under channel estimation errors. Both approaches demonstrate performance gains over systems that rely solely on either near‑field or far‑field models, validating that hybrid designs enable seamless connectivity and sensing for heterogeneous industrial devices with manageable computational overhead.



