\subsubsection{Enhanced ISAC with NOMA and RIS}
Recent works have increasingly leveraged RIS and NOMA to expand the design space of ISAC systems. Notably, \cite{Xue2025noma_isac2} introduces a hybrid NOMA-ISAC framework that adaptively manages interference based on user and antenna configurations, and jointly optimizes power allocation, decoding order and transmit beamforming. Regarding the RIS-assisted ISAC systems, the authors in \cite{Ruizhang_ISAC} minimize the CRB for multi‑user target estimation by jointly optimizing the base station's transmit beamforming and the RIS phase shifts under per‑user SINR constraints, yielding significant performance gains over separate beamforming and random-phase baselines. In \cite{noma_ris_isac_1}, a comprehensive RIS-NOMA-ISAC framework is investigated by jointly optimizing base station beamforming, power allocation among NOMA users and RIS phase shifts, which can have the prominent advantages over RIS-enabled ISAC or SO systems in terms of beam pattern gain.

\subsubsection{Cooperative Bandwidth Splitting ISAC}
Conventional ISAC implementations typically allocate the entire available bandwidth solely for ISAC operation. However, this approach could be challenging in real applications since the legacy high‑frequency bands are pre-assigned for dedicated sensing operations only \cite{Griffiths2015RadarSpectrum}. To address this limitation, \cite{Bliss} introduces a joint‑utilization paradigm that aggregates the existing sensing‑only (SO) band with a dedicated ISAC band, thereby enabling enhanced sensing accuracy and communication throughput under spectral coexistence conditions. 
This concept is further extended in \cite{Semi_ISAC, partialNOMA2023Akhtar}, where a bandwidth splitting ratio is introduced to partition the spectrum into communication-only, SO and ISAC bands. By incorporating uplink NOMA, these works can control the interference between the sensing and communication signals and demonstrate improved sensing accuracy and communication performance compared to conventional ISAC band-only or OMA schemes. However, the existing studies \cite{Bliss, Xu2024FullISAC, Semi_ISAC} remain restricted to performance analysis under predefined bandwidth splitting settings, without optimizing the ratio for optimal performance.

To the best of our knowledge, as shown in Table \ref{related works}, this is the first work that investigates a RIS-assisted ISAC for the 6G IIoT to account for the near-field and far-field coexistence. With the various deployments of IIoT devices and RIS, we explore the possible coexistence scenarios of near-field and far-field regions, providing insight into the practical implementations and inspiring more innovations in the future IIoT.  
