\subsection{Impact of RIS Horizontal Position on Near/Far Field Transition and NOMA}
To investigate the impact of NOMA decoding performance and the transition between near-field and far-field channel conditions, Fig. \ref{NOMA_result} demonstrates the estimation error as a function of the RIS’s $x$-coordinate. In the simulation set-up, the RIS horizontal position is varied such that the AP–RIS and RIS–IIoT-(II) devices links undergo transitions between near-field and far-field propagation regimes. Specifically, for $20\text{m}\leq x\leq30\text{m}$
, the AP–RIS link is in the near-field, while the RIS–IIoT-(II) devices link is in the far-field; in the range $35\text{m}\leq x\leq45\text{m}$, both links are in the near-field; and for $50\text{m}\leq x\leq55\text{m}$, the AP–RIS link becomes far-field, while the RIS–IIoT-(II) link remains near-field. It is observed that the worst sensing estimation performance occurs around $x=40$m, where severe multiplicative fading through the RIS substantially degrades the system performance.
The \textit{Random RIS} scheme shows considerable performance fluctuations and degradations due to the lack of optimized RIS beamforming. The proposed \textit{SO \& ISAC} method outperforms all benchmarks across the entire RIS deployment range by adaptively optimizing the RIS phase-shifts and receive beamforming and adjusting the bandwidth splitting ratio. Specifically, the proposed method shows the largest performance gap in the range $25\text{m}\leq x\leq30\text{m}$ where the AP and RIS are closely located. In the fully near-field region, e.g., $35\text{m}\leq x\leq45\text{m}$, the accurate beam alignment becomes critical, and lacking phase-shift optimization in \textit{Random RIS} hence causes larger performance swings. When the RIS approaches the IIoT-(II) devices in the $50\text{m}\leq x\leq55\text{m}$ range, system performance improves due to more favorable proximity. This performance variation ultimately reflects the sensitivity of NOMA decoding and near-field and far-field transition according to the RIS position, as the rate constraints imposed by IIoT-(II) devices directly affect the residual interference.

%Fig. \ref{NOMA_result}는 ris의 위치변화에 따른 estimation error를 보여준다. 시뮬레이션 상에서 20m-30m 구간에서 AP-RIS channel은 near-field RIS-IIoT channel은 far-field, 35m-45m 구간에서 AP-RIS channel은 near-field RIS-IIoT channel은 near-field, 그리고 50m-55m 구간에서 AP-RIS channel은 far-field RIS-IIoT channel은 near-field이다. 전체적으로 ris의 multiplicative fading의 영향을 많이 받는 ris의 x-coordinate가 45m 부근에서 가장 시스템성능이 안좋은 모습을 보인다. \textbf{Full SO}에서 ISAC band만을 사용하기 때문에 ris의 x 좌표가 변함에 따라 시스템 퍼포먼스의 변화가 미미함을 확인할 수 있다. 반면에, \textbf{Random RIS}에서는 상대적으로 시스템 퍼포먼스의 변화율이 큰 것을 확인할 수 있다. RIS-IIoT channel이 farfield구간인 x 좌표 25m-30m에서는 ap와 ris의 위치가 가깝고 band split ratio 최적화를 통해 성능의 향상을 확인할 수 있다. 또한 RIS-IIoT channel이 near-field 구간인 35m-55m에서는 전송신호의 빔패턴이 narrow beam으로 형성되어 정확한 방향성이 중요하기 때문에 ris phase shift를 최적화 하지 않은 \textbf{Random RIS}는 성능의 변화폭이 큰 것을 확인할 수 있다. 마찬가지로,RIS와 IIoT device와 가까워 지는 50m-55m구간에서는 성능의 증가를 확인할 수 있다. 제안된 모델은 벤치마크들과 비교했을 때, 전 구간에서 가장 우수한 시스템 퍼포먼스를 보여준다. ris phase shift를 최적화 함으로써 near-field구간에서 안정적인 성능을 보여주며, band split ratio 최적화를 통해서 RIS-IIOT channel이 far-field구간에서 성능의 향상을 보여준다.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{Conclusion}
In this paper, we have investigated a RIS-assisted ISAC framework designed for IIoT scenarios with near-field and far-field coexistence. To address the practical constraints of industrial environments, we consider a resource allocation strategy that assigns both ISAC band and the SO band. In addition, a NOMA-based uplink communication protocol is adopted to manage multiple types of IIoT devices, facilitating sequential decoding of communication and sensing signals. 

To enhance the overall system performance, the joint design is proposed to minimize the CRB of time-delay estimation by optimizing the RIS phase shifts, bandwidth splitting ratio, and receive beamforming, subject to data rate requirements determined by the types of IIoT devices. The optimization procedure is efficiently handled using an SCA-based AO algorithm combined with SDR techniques. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed scheme achieves superior estimation accuracy compared to relying solely on either ISAC or SO band, using equal bandwidth for both bands, or employing random RIS phase shifts. Moreover, the adaptive resource and beamforming management yields robust performance improvements under varying NOMA and near-field and far-field channel conditions. The future research can leverage a learning-based approach to efficiently manage a massive number of IIoT devices and further enhance sensing performance.


