\section{Limitations and Future Work}
\label{appendix:limitation}

Our approach focuses on bilateral cultural negotiation scenarios and has been validated through experiments. 
However, real-world communication and collaboration sometimes exceed two-party interactions, involving multiple parties or scenarios with evolving participant numbers and roles. 
Compared with bilateral negotiations, multi-party interactions extend relationship networks, introduce intricate benefit structures and alliances, and pose additional demands on decision-making strategies and utility function design~\cite{Kozina2020MultipartyNC}. 
In response, we plan to extend our framework by introducing utility function designs for multi-party negotiations, alliance formation mechanisms, and unified platform protocols that allow participants to negotiate and collaborate in one environment~\cite{10.5555/1630659.1630877, 10.1007/s00766-021-00368-y}. 
Fields such as diplomacy, policy-making with multiple actors, and decision-making serve as fields of application for validating the robustness and generality of this multi-party negotiation approach, moving it toward broader usage~\cite{SOLINAS2022102240, REN201429}.

At present, our development of regional cultural agents relies on survey data or cultural texts for value information. Although these sources capture a community's values at a given time, values evolve with changes in social structures, economic conditions~\cite{Lehman2021-rd}, and historical events~\cite{qiuProgressGymAlignmentMillennium2024}. Sustaining accuracy and interpretability over extended periods or in shifting contexts therefore requires more recent and varied data. To address this, we will collaborate with disciplines such as sociology and psychology to identify broad trends in value evolution, enabling more precise modeling. We will also explore methods that integrate multiple fields, broadening data scope and timeliness, which will enhance the scale and future applicability of regional value modeling~\cite{Lavi2024-ov}. As culture-related research findings accumulate across different eras and domains, our framework will continue to evolve, allowing cultural agents to adapt to developments and changes in real-world societies.

Due to GPU and API resource constraints, we are unable to run the full set of experiments across all 28 culture pairs. Instead, we select seven representative culture-pair scenarios for evaluation. As additional computational and API resources become available, we plan to extend our experimental scope to cover the remaining culture combinations, thereby providing more comprehensive and statistically robust validation of our framework.


\section{Social Impact}

\label{appendix:social_impact}

The global adoption of large language models (LLMs) affects collective cognition and shapes social values. Although these models can influence many processes, they often reflect biases rooted in WEIRD (Western, Educated, Industrialized, Rich, and Democratic) contexts, which can sideline non-Western perspectives~\cite{agarwalEthicalReasoningMoral2024, benklerAssessingLLMsMoral2023, liuCulturalBiasLarge2024, quPerformanceBiasesLarge2024}. These biases may deepen when models are used in policy-making, international relations, or resource allocation, where dialogue and understanding are essential~\cite{aoki2024largelanguagemodelspolitics, jiangEditorialLargeLanguage2025}. By configuring negotiation frameworks to incorporate varied cultural norms and preferences, our approach advances AI-driven consensus-building that includes more voices.

Nevertheless, it is necessary to recognize the opportunities and threats associated with deploying AI systems aligned with cultural practices. Although our system aims to yield balanced outcomes and include underrepresented cultural voices, it might inadvertently reinforce dominant viewpoints~\cite{aoki2024largelanguagemodelspolitics, Kaashoek2024Impact}. If introduced without transparent oversight or ethical controls, automated cultural negotiation processes may be exploited to consolidate prevailing dominant narratives or favor majoritarian agendas, thereby silencing minority cultures. These concerns highlight the need for responsible innovation, supported by measures such as fairness reviews and inclusive data collection, to limit potential misuse~\cite{Huang_2024}.


\textbf{We call for a responsible approach to the culture alignment technologies of AI, recognizing the possible harms and taking steps to prevent misuse.}
We urge that these technologies should be designed and deployed from the outset without using their influence to reinforce majority cultures or suppress minority ones~\cite{Huang_2024}. By following principles of diversity and inclusion, data sources and algorithmic mechanisms should be structured to reflect a range of cultural perspectives~\cite{Roche2023-dq}. In practice, frameworks grounded in ethics and policy must be established to monitor and correct any undesired outcomes~\cite{Attard-Frost2023-wb}. When facilitating cultural exchange or negotiation, these technologies should not function as instruments of one-sided communication or persuasion. Instead, they should support cross-cultural understanding and dialogue to enable participation and mutual development. Finally, while fostering innovation and extending the reach of technology, it is necessary to safeguard the rights and continuity of cultures worldwide, ensuring progress that is inclusive and guided by accountability.

