\subsection{Hofstede Cultural Dimensions}
\label{appendix:tool_hofstede_cultural_dimensions}

Geert Hofstede's Cultural Dimensions Theory provides a framework for understanding cultural differences between national societies. Developed through comparative analysis of matched country samples using the Values Survey Module (VSM)\cite{kharchenko2024llmsrepresentvaluescultures,masoud2024culturalalignmentlargelanguage,masoud2025culturalalignmentlargelanguage}, it identifies six fundamental cultural continua that shape societal norms and workplace behaviors. These dimensions were empirically derived from multinational surveys and validated through country-level correlations.

\subsection*{The Six Cultural Dimensions}

\begin{description}

  \item[Power Distance Index (PDI)] PDI measures the extent to which less powerful members of institutions accept and expect unequal power distribution. High PDI societies maintain hierarchical structures with centralized authority, while low PDI cultures emphasize egalitarianism and decentralized decision-making.

  \item[Individualism vs. Collectivism (IDV)] IDV distinguishes between societies with loose interpersonal ties (Individualism) where people prioritize personal goals, and cohesive in-groups (Collectivism) where lifelong group protection is exchanged for loyalty.

  \item[Masculinity vs. Femininity (MAS)] MAS contrasts achievement-oriented societies emphasizing material success and assertiveness (Masculinity) with nurturing cultures valuing quality of life and interpersonal harmony (Femininity).

  \item[Uncertainty Avoidance Index (UAI)] UAI indicates a society's tolerance for ambiguous situations. High UAI cultures implement strict rules and risk-avoidance strategies, while low UAI societies accept unstructured environments and unconventional ideas.

  \item[Long-Term vs. Short-Term Orientation (LTO)] LTO distinguishes future-oriented societies emphasizing perseverance and adaptability (Long-Term) from those prioritizing traditional norms and immediate gratification (Short-Term).

  \item[Indulgence vs. Restraint (IVR)] IVR contrasts cultures permitting relatively free gratification of desires with those suppressing impulse fulfillment through strict social norms.
\end{description}


\subsection{Schwartz's Theory of Basic Values}
\label{appendix:tool_schwartz's_theory_of_basic_values}
Schwartz's Theory of Basic Values \cite{OverviewSchwartzTheory, schwartzUniversalsContentStructure1992} offers a universal framework for analyzing the cultural context of a sentence by categorizing human values according to their underlying guiding principles. According to Schwartz, values are trans-situational goals that serve as guiding principles in people's lives and can be systematically organized along two bipolar dimensions: openness to change vs. conservation, and self-enhancement vs. self-transcendence. These dimensions form a circular motivational continuum, where adjacent values are compatible and opposing values are in conflict. The model identifies ten basic value types-including \textit{self-direction, stimulation, achievement, hedonism, power, conformity, tradition, benevolence and universalism}-that are recognized across cultures and can be used to compare individual or societal value orientations. Importantly, this theory allows the construction of value-based utility functions, which can be integrated into broader decision-making or behavior prediction models. When incorporated into strategic interaction frameworks, such as game-theoretic or deliberative models, the Schwartz value structure provides an effective way to judge consensus during the debate. 


\section{Details of Cross-Cultural Negotiation Method}
\label{appendix:method}

\subsection{Formalization}
The cultural negotiation process is modeled as a two-player extensive-form game, formally defined by the quintuple: $\Gamma \doteq \langle \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{G}, \mathcal{W}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{H} \rangle$
, where:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Cultural Entities:} $\mathcal{I} \doteq \{A, B\}$, the set of two distinct cultural entities involved in the negotiation, where $A$ and $B$ represent different cultures with their own values and perspectives.
    \item \textbf{Guideline Sets:} $\mathcal{G} \doteq \{G_i | i \in \mathcal{I}\}$, each guideline $g \in G_i$ is structured as a triple $g = \langle \text{content}, \text{reason}, \text{description} \rangle$, where \textit{content} articulates the core cultural claim, \textit{reason} provides the justificatory foundation within the cultural framework, and \textit{description} provides a detailed explanation. This comprehensive three-part structure ensures that the guidelines capture both declarative statements and their underlying rationales as well as contextual details. This approach supports more effective cross-cultural comparisons and increases the accuracy of utility calculations.
    \item \textbf{Guideline Weights:} $\mathcal{W} \doteq \{W_i | i \in \mathcal{I}\}$, for each culture $i \in \mathcal{I}$, $W_i \in \Delta(G_i)$ denotes a probability distribution over its guidelines, with $\sum_g w_i(g) = 1$. $W_i$ thus characterizes the expressive emphasis of culture $i$ in the current negotiation round. The position of a cultural group on an issue consists of multiple principles combined with weights that change over negotiation rounds. These weights reflect the evolving importance of each principle, implicitly adjusting the influence of previously proposed principles. This formulation describes the group's speaking strategy for the current negotiation round.
\item \textbf{Utility Functions:} $\mathcal{U} \doteq \{U_i | i \in \mathcal{I}\}$, quantify the utility each culture derives from different guideline combinations.
\item \textbf{Negotiation History:} $\mathcal{H}$records all negotiation trajectories as$h: \{y_{A, 1}, y_{B, 1},y_{A, 2},y_{B, 2}\} \in \mathcal{H}$, where each round features two utterances per agent: 1) \textbf{Strategy Presentation:} Articulating current strategy (guideline weights distribution). 2) \textbf{New Claim Proposal:} Formulating updated claims in response to opponent's strategy.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Negotiation Process}
Figure~\ref{fig:framework} shows the complete process of our method.
\subsubsection{Phase 1: Initialization}

\textbf{Initial Guideline Sets:} Establish the foundational cultural guidelines for each cultural entity. For culture $i$, the initial guideline set is represented as $G_i^0 = \{g_{i,1}^0, \dots, g_{i,k}^0\}$. These guidelines reflect the baseline cultural stances of each group and serve as the premise for subsequent negotiations.

\textbf{Utility Matrix Construction:} Construct the cross-cultural utility matrix by calculating the initial utilities $u_i(g_i, g_{-i}), \forall g_{i,k} \in G_i^0, \forall i \in \mathcal{I}$. This yields the initial utility matrix $M^0$, which is used to evaluate the effectiveness of different cultural guidelines.

\textbf{Initial Weight Allocation:} Allocate initial weights using a uniform distribution across the guidelines, denoted as $W_i^0 = \text{Uniform}(G_i^0)$. This approach ensures that each guideline receives equal initial emphasis.

\subsubsection{Parse 2: Negotiation Iteration}
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{imgs/new_debate_framework.pdf}
    \caption{Overview of our PSRO-based cross-cultural negotiation method.}
    \label{fig:framework}
\end{figure}
After initialization, the two cultural agents begin negotiation. Each round consists of the following steps:

\textbf{Interim Consensus:} The objective of this phase is to reach an interim consensus, where both players find a balanced agreement based on their current positions.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textit{Cultural Equilibrium Computation:} Complete any missing entries in the utility matrix and use Mirror Descent (Appendex~\ref{appendix:method_nash_eq_compute}) to derive the Nash equilibrium weight distributions $(\mathcal{W}_A^t, \mathcal{W}_B^t)$ for the current guideline sets. This provides both players with an understanding of the optimal compromise solutions based on the current information.

    \item \textit{Weight Update Mechanism:} Apply a smoothed update mechanism to prevent extreme weight allocations, represented as $w'_i = \gamma \cdot \text{Uniform}(n) + (1-\gamma) \cdot w_i$.

    \item \textit{First Speech:} Describe the guideline weight distribution using a rule-based approach (Appendex~\ref{appendix:method_weight_desc}). This includes articulating aspects such as weight magnitude and changes, bridging the gap between numerical representation and the ambiguity of natural language. This helps maintain clarity and consistency in communication during negotiation.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{New Guideline Proposal:} After reaching an interim consensus, both players propose new guidelines to advance the negotiation process. Refer to Appendix~\ref{appendix:method_br} for details.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textit{Opponent Strategy Analysis:} Analyze the opponent's current strategies to identify weaknesses or limitations. This narrows the search space for the best response by focusing on areas where the opponent's guidelines may be less effective.

    \item \textit{New Candidates Generation:} Generate diverse candidate guidelines based on the analysis. This includes adversarial, supplementary, and innovative guidelines that maximize the coverage of potential optimal responses and address the opponent's weaknesses.

    \item \textit{Guideline Evaluation and Selection:} Select the candidate guideline with the highest expected utility as the best response (BR), that is, $g_i^{t+1} = \arg\max_{g'} \mathbb{E}_{g_{-i} \sim W_{-i}^t} \left[ U_i(g', g_{-i}) \right]$. If the new guideline yields a sufficient utility gain, i.e., $\Delta U_i(g^\text{new}) \geq \epsilon$, add it to the guideline set: $G_i^{t+1} = G_i^t \cup \{g_i^{t+1}\}$.

    \item \textit{Second Speech:} Use natural language to describe the new guideline.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Parse 3: Final Consensus}

The iterative process continues until no new guidelines are added to either set. At this stage, the final guideline weights $(W_A^\ast, W_B^\ast)$ are established, representing the cross-cultural consensus achieved through negotiation. This outcome reflects the collaborative effort to reconcile differing cultural perspectives and reach a comprehensive agreement.

\subsection{Introduction to PSRO}

In multi-agent settings, strategic interactions are modeled in one of two classic forms. In a normal form game, all players move simultaneously and payoffs are captured in matrices. In an extensive form game, sequential decisions and information sets are represented by a tree. A key concept is the best response (BR): given fixed strategies for all other players, a BR is any strategy that maximizes a player's payoff.

Independent reinforcement learning (InRL)~\cite{lee2021investigationindependentreinforcementlearning, lanctotUnifiedGameTheoreticApproach2017} treats other agents as part of a nonstationary environment. Using InRL can overfit to the other agents' policies during training, failing to sufficiently generalize during execution.

Traditional solutions such as Double Oracle (DO)~\cite{10.5555/2030470.2030518} use repeated subgame solves to approximate an equilibrium in the full game. At each iteration, it solves for an equilibrium in a restricted subgame defined by a small set of strategies and then adds each player's BR to that equilibrium back into the strategy set. Although DO has convergence guarantees, its worst case complexity grows exponentially with the size of the underlying strategy space.

Policy Sspace Response Oracles (PSRO)~\cite{lanctotUnifiedGameTheoreticApproach2017} extends the DO from normal form games to extensive form games and addresses the shortcomings of InRL. Instead of computing exact BRs, we compute approximate BRs using reinforcement learning. PSRO maintains for each agent a pool of past policies. At each iteration, a meta solver (a Nash equilibrium solver) computes a mixed strategy equilibrium over the current pools. Then, for each player, a BR oracle trains a new policy that best responds to the opponents' meta strategy and adds this policy to the pool. This loop continues until convergence. Algorithm~\ref{alg:psro} presents the PSRO workflow.

In structured debate, each PSRO iteration corresponds to a cycle of argument generation, counterargument, and consensus adjustment, driving the policy space toward an equilibrium of perspectives.




\begin{algorithm}[H]
\label{alg:psro}
\caption{Policy-Space Response Oracles (PSRO)}
\KwIn{Game $G$; meta-solver MSS; best-response oracle BR; iterations $T$}
\KwOut{Strategy pools $\{\Pi_i\}$ and meta-strategy $\sigma$}

Initialize $\Pi_i \gets \{\pi_i^0\}$ for all players $i$; set $\sigma_i(\pi_i^0)=1$; simulate initial payoff tensor $U^0$\;
\For{$t = 0$ \KwTo $T-1$}{
    \For{each player $i$}{
        $\Pi_i \gets \Pi_i \cup \{\mathrm{BR}(i,\sigma)\}$\;
    }
    Simulate payoffs for all new policy profiles to update $U^t$\;
    $\sigma \gets \mathrm{MSS}(U^t)$\;
}
\Return{$\{\Pi_i\},\,\sigma$}
\end{algorithm}


