We select topics by screening and rephrasing items from the Pew Global Attitudes Survey (GAS)~\cite{PewGlobalAttitudesSurvey2014, durmus2024measuringrepresentationsubjectiveglobal} and the World Values Survey (WVS)~\cite{WVS_Wave7, durmus2024measuringrepresentationsubjectiveglobal}. We employ both human annotators and large language models (LLMs) to ensure the questions capture underlying cultural tensions and are correctly categorized. The annotation procedure comprises four steps:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Removing questions with global consensus or limited relevance to cultural divides.
    \item Reformulating the remaining items into clear, debate-oriented prompts for cross-cultural comparison.
    \item Assigning each prompt to a single category based on its primary dimension of conflict.
    \item Validating both category assignments and prompt wording via multi-annotator agreement and LLM consistency checks.
\end{enumerate}

