\textbf{Calculation of Hofstede Dimensions}


Scores are calculated using standardized formulas from the VSM 2013 manual:
\begin{align*}
  \textit{PDI} &= 35 \cdot (m_{07} - m_{02}) + 25 \cdot (m_{20} - m_{23}) + C_{\text{pdi}} \\
  \textit{IDV} &= 35 \cdot (m_{04} - m_{01}) + 35 \cdot (m_{09} - m_{06}) + C_{\text{idv}} \\
  \textit{MAS} &= 35 \cdot (m_{05} - m_{03}) + 35 \cdot (m_{08} - m_{10}) + C_{\text{mas}} \\
  \textit{UAI} &= 40 \cdot (m_{18} - m_{15}) + 25 \cdot (m_{21} - m_{24}) + C_{\text{uai}} \\
  \textit{LTO} &= 40 \cdot (m_{13} - m_{14}) + 25 \cdot (m_{19} - m_{22}) + C_{\text{lto}} \\
  \textit{IVR} &= 35 \cdot (m_{12} - m_{11}) + 40 \cdot (m_{17} - m_{16}) + C_{\text{ivr}} \\
\end{align*}
\noindent
where \( m_{01} \) to \( m_{24} \) represent mean scores of VSM content questions (on 1-5 scales), and constants \( C \) normalize each index to a 0-100 scale for cross-national comparability.
\subsection{Response-Level Evaluation}
We adopt two methods to evaluate whether a debate leads to a consensus: a Perplexity(PPL)-based Acceptance approach and a model-based Value Self-Consistency approach.
\label{appendix:PPL_based_evaluation}
\subsubsection{PPL-based Acceptence}
\textbf{Exchanging Roles for Response Generation} To assess the probability that each agent generates the other's statements during the debate, two agents exchange roles---each agent is tasked with producing the response intended for the other. Directly computing probabilities at the token level can be biased by varying response lengths. To mitigate length effects, we employ a Perplexity (PPL) metric.

\textbf{Determining Consensus} As the debate progresses, we track how the probability difference of each agent generating the opponent's responses changes over time. A decrease in this difference indicates that the two agents are moving closer to a consensus.

\textbf{Perplexity Formula} For each culture $i \in \mathcal{I}$ and its counterpart $-i$, let $x_i$ denote the agent's input,  $y_i$ the agent's response and $x_i, y_{-i}$ the opponent's. The agents swap roles to regenerate each other's responses. The perplexity is computed as:
\begin{equation}
   \operatorname{Perplexity}_i(y_{-i}) =
  \exp\!\Bigl(
    -\frac{1}{N}
    \sum_{k=1}^{N}
    \log p(y_{-i,k} \mid y_{-i,<k}, x_{-i})
  \Bigr) 
\end{equation}
where $N$ is the sequence length, and $p(y_{-i, k} \mid y_{-i, <k}, x_{-i})$ is the probability of generating the opponent's $k$-th token given the preceding tokens $y_{<k}$ and $x$.

\textbf{Perplexity Distance} In a debate scenario, each agent has both an initial and a final response,
so we define the PPL distance at initial ($0$) and consensus ($*$, or final) response as:
$$
\operatorname{Perplexity}_{\Delta}^0 = \bigl| \operatorname{Perplexity}_i^0(y_{-i}) - \operatorname{Perplexity}_{-i}^0(y_{i}) \bigr|
$$
$$
\operatorname{Perplexity}_{\Delta}^* = \bigl| \operatorname{Perplexity}_i^*(y_{-i}) - \operatorname{Perplexity}_{-i}^*(y_{i}) \bigr|
$$
The Perplexity-based acceptance ratio is:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Perplexity}_{\text{acc}} = \frac{\operatorname{Perplexity}_{\Delta}^*}{\operatorname{Perplexity}_{\Delta}^0}
\end{equation}

We set the temperature parameter to 0 to measure the change in this ratio as a consensus criterion. If $\operatorname{Perplexity}_{\text{acc}} < 1$, then the final distance is less than the initial distance, showing that the agents have aligned and thus reached consensus.

We collect $\operatorname{Perplexity}_{\text{acc}}$ across all negotiation instances, and define the overall acceptance score as the proportion of cases satisfying :
\begin{equation}    
\text{Perplexity-Acceptance Score} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|} \sum_{t \in \mathcal{T}} \mathbb{I}\!\left[\operatorname{Perplexity}_{\text{acc}}^{(t)} < 1\right]
\end{equation}

where $\mathcal{T}$ denotes the set of all evaluated negotiation topics.
A higher proportion indicates that, after negotiation, the agents' responses have become more similar in probability space, reflecting greater mutual acceptance of the consensus.

\subsubsection{Value Self-Consistency}
\label{appendix:model_based_evaluation}

\textbf{Dimension Agreement}\\
Schwartz's Theory of Basic Values categorizes human values into 10 dimensions. Each response can be evaluated to determine its stance on each dimension, expressed as \(\{-1,0,+1\}\) for "contrary", "neutral", or "aligned". Consequently, each response is represented as a 10-dimensional vector:
\begin{equation}
V(r) = \bigl(v_1(r),\,v_2(r),\,\dots,\,v_{10}(r)\bigr),
\end{equation}
where \(v_i(r)\in\{-1,0,+1\}\). 


\textbf{Value Self-Consistency (VSC)}\\
Value Self-Consistency (VSC) quantifies the extent to which a single party maintains its value alignment from an initial response to a final (consensus) response. Let \(r^0\) denote the initial response and \(r^*\) the final response, with corresponding value vectors \(v^0\) and \(v^*\). The VSC is defined as:

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{VSC}
= \text{DimensionAgreement}(r^0, r^*)
= \frac{1}{10}\sum_{i=1}^{10}\mathbb{I}\bigl[v_i(r^0)=v_i(r^*)\bigr].
\end{equation}

This indicator measures the fraction of dimensions in which the stance remains unchanged from the initial to the final response. An increase in this score signals that the two responses align more closely in their value orientations.

\textbf{Summary Statistics Based on VSC}

Let $\mathcal{T}$ denote the set of all negotiation topics. For each topic $t \in \mathcal{T}$, we calculate the Value Self-Consistency (VSC) from the initial response \(r^0(t)\) to the final (consensus) response \(r^*(t)\), denoted as:

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{VSC}(t) 
= \frac{1}{10}\sum_{i=1}^{10} \mathbb{I}\bigl[v_i(r^0(t)) = v_i(r^*(t))\bigr],
\end{equation}

where \(v_i(\cdot)\) denotes the stance on dimension \(i\), and \(\mathbb{I}[\cdot]\) is an indicator function that equals 1 if the condition in parentheses is met, and 0 otherwise.

Next, to assess the extent to which the negotiation method preserves value orientation consistency overall, we compute the average VSC across all topics:
\begin{equation}
\overline{\mathrm{VSC}}
= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|} \sum_{t \in \mathcal{T}} \mathrm{VSC}(t).
\end{equation}

This average, \(\overline{\mathrm{VSC}}\), reflects the overall proportion of dimensions for which participants' stances remain unchanged from the initial to the final response during the negotiation. A higher \(\overline{\mathrm{VSC}}\) indicates that in most topics, the value orientations in the initial and final responses are more closely aligned, suggesting that the negotiation process generally preserves the participants' value orientations.


