%
%

One could hypothesize our success against the M-Score is achieved only due to a large number of terms and thus complex interactions between them, as each accounting variable in \autoref{eq:m-score} is composed of the sum of many atoms and thus creates more of the non-linearities that are exploited in neural networks for evasive purposes. By showing that the S-Score also succumbs to our MVMO algorithms, we show a three-way joint optimization and that simpler fraud models are equally at risk. 

