The following inductive definition gives the basic dynamic programming formulation to compute $\overline{V}_0 F=\overline{U}_0F$ (see Definition 9 from \cite{degano}). %In the following definition, $\overline{U}_iF$ is defined on $\mathcal{X}$ while $\mathcal{X}_{(X,i)}^1$ refers to the conditional space of one dimensional trajectories sharing the first $i+1$ coordinates with $X^1.$ 

\begin{definition}[Dynamic Bounds] \label{dynamicBounds}
For a given
 $X\in \mathcal{X}$, and
$0\le i \le n= N(X)$ set
\begin{equation}  \label{DynamicBound}
\overline{U}_i F(X)= \left\lbrace \begin{array}{lcc}
\inf\limits_{H\in\mathcal{H}} \sup\limits_{\hat{X} \in \mathcal{X}_{(X,i)}}
[\overline{U}_{i+1}F(\hat{X}) - H_i(X)
\Delta_i \hat{X}^1] &\mbox{if}& 0\le i< n,\\
F(X) &\mbox{if}& i=n,\\
0 &\mbox{if}& i>n,
\end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_i\hat{X}^1=\hat{X}_{i+1}^1-\hat{X}^1_i$ and $\hat{X}\in\mathcal{X}_{(X,i)}$. % are elements of the conditional space containing one dimensional trajectories sharing the first $i+1$ coordinates with $X^1.$ 
