\abstract{We study networks of processes that all execute the same finite-state protocol and communicate via broadcasts. We are interested in two problems with a parameterized number of processes: the synchronization problem which asks whether there is an execution which puts all processes on a given state; and the repeated coverability problem which asks if there is an infinite execution where a given transition is taken infinitely often. Since both problems are undecidable in the general case, we investigate those problems when the protocol is \emph{Wait-Only}, i.e., it has no state from which a process can both broadcast and receive messages. We establish that the synchronization problem becomes Ackermann-complete, and the repeated coverability problem is in \expspace\,  and \pspace-hard.}

