\subsection{Equilibrium states derived from variational principles}
\label{sec:equilibrium_theories}

Substantial efforts have been devoted to predicting the equilibrium states of 2D turbulent flows, either with or without topography, based on variational principles such as those of minimum enstrophy and maximum entropy. These variational principles yield some functional relations between the PV and the streamfunction, expressed as
\begin{equation}
    q = f(\psi).
\end{equation}
Such a functional relation defines exact stationary solutions to the inviscid QG PV equation.
In numerical simulations, an empirical PV-streamfunction relation can be obtained by inspecting the scatter plots between the PV and the streamfunction after a long-time integration, which can then be compared against theoretical predictions.
In what follows, we review two equilibrium theories that are relevant to our work and extend one of the theories to our QG topographic configuration.

\subsubsection{Minimum-enstrophy theory}
A well-established equilibrium theory of 2D topographic turbulence was proposed by \citet{brethertonTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography1976} (BH hereafter) based on the minimum-enstrophy principle.
% It is observed that, in 2D decaying turbulence, the energy is nearly conserved during time while the total enstrophy drops dramatically.
BH conjectured that 2D decaying turbulence would evolve into a state of minimum total enstrophy, while conserving the total energy.
The minimum-enstrophy state can be obtained by performing variational operations to the functional $\mathcal{L}=Q+\mu E$.
The result is a linear relation between the PV and the streamfunction, namely,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:linear_relation}
    q = \Delta\psi + \eta =\mu\psi,
\end{equation}
with the linear slope $\mu$ serving as the Lagrange multiplier that constrains the energy.
For the two topographies shown in figure \ref{fig:topography}, the minimum-enstrophy states can be obtained by solving the above equation.
The corresponding energy and total enstrophy are
\refstepcounter{equation}\label{eq:minimumenstrophy_energy_entrophy}
$$
E_*=\frac{1}{2(\mu+1)^2}; \quad Q_*=\frac{\mu^2}{2(\mu+1)^2}, \eqno{(\theequation{\mathit{a},\mathit{b}})}
$$
respectively.


A critical case arises when $\mu=0$, in which the PV is homogenenised ($q_\#=0$).
The corresponding streamfunction $\psi_\#$, also called topographic streamfunction \citep{siegelmanTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2023}, is obtained by applying the inverse Laplacian operator to the topography, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:topographic_streamfunction}
    \psi_\# = -\Delta^{-1}\eta.
\end{equation}
The corresponding energy $E_\#$ was considered as a reference for separating the high- and low-energy levels \citep{siegelmanTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2023}. 
For the two topographies shown in figure \ref{fig:topography}, the critical energy $E_\#$ is simply $0.5$, according to (\ref{eq:minimumenstrophy_energy_entrophy}$a$).
Throughout this work, we are interested in the low-energy regime ($E<E_\#$), since vortices in this regime are topographically-locked, resembling those in the realistic ocean \citep{kohlGenerationStabilityQuasiPermanent2007,solodochFormationAnticyclonesTopographic2021}. 
% and correlated to topography with the cyclone and anticyclone above the respective maximum and minimum positions.
We fix the energy level as $E=0.25E_\#$, at which the linear slope $\mu$ predicted by the minimum-enstrophy theory is $1$.

Recent numerical simulations uncovered general deviations of the final states of 2D topographic turbulence from the minimum-enstrophy solutions, due to the presence of long-lived and isolated vortices, although the theoretical minima of total enstrophy can be approached with very large-scale initial conditions \citep{galletTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2024,heMultipleStatesTwodimensional2024}.
The resulting $q$--$\psi$ relations are generally nonlinear and possess many branches that correspond to different vortices (see scatter plots in the supplementary materials in \citet{siegelmanTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2023}).
It is this discrepancy between the minimum-enstrophy states and the numerical simulations that motivates us to consider an alternative organising principle, detailed below. 

\subsubsection{Maximum-entropy theory}
The theories of maximum entropy are built upon applying the equilibrium statistical mechanics to 2D inviscid flows.
A maximum-entropy theory directly relevant to 2D topographic turbulence was developed by \citet{salmonEquilibriumStatisticalMechanics1976} based on truncated quasi-geostrophic equations.
It also yields a linear $q$--$\psi$ relation similar to that in the minimum-enstrophy theory.
The equivalence between the two theories have been demonstrated by \citet{carnevaleNonlinearStabilityStatistical1987} in the limit of infinite resolution.
Because of this, the maximum-entropy theory of \citet{salmonEquilibriumStatisticalMechanics1976} also fails to describe the long-lived vortices observed in numerical simulations and is hence not considered further.

An alternative maximum-entropy theory dealing specifically with  vortices was developed by \citet{joyceNegative1973} and \citet{MontgomeryStatistical1974} for 2D flat-bottom turbulence based on the point-vortex model of \citet{onsagerStatisticalHydrodynamics1949}.
Modern reviews can be found in \citet{eyinkOnsagerTheoryHydrodynamic2006} and \citet{eyink2014turbulence}.
This theory assumes that the vorticity field can be modelled by an ensemble of point vortices with zero circulation, in which positive vortices are antisymmetric to their negative counterparts.
Such a system is Hamiltonian and conserves energy.
The most probable distribution of point vortices is obtained by maximising the entropy, subject to the constraint of fixed energy.
The entropy is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:entropy}
    S(\zeta_+,\zeta_-) = -\iint \zeta_+(x,y)\ln{\zeta_+(x,y)}dxdy - \iint \zeta_-(x,y)\ln{\zeta_-(x,y)}dxdy,
\end{equation}
where $\zeta_+(x,y)$ and $\zeta_-(x,y)$ ($\zeta_\pm\geq0$) are the densities of positive and negative point vortices, respectively.
The vorticity field is recovered by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:vorticity_by_densities}
    \zeta(x,y) = \zeta_+(x,y) - \zeta_-(x,y).
\end{equation}
The total energy in this case is written in terms of the vorticity field as
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:energy_by_vorticity}
    E(\zeta) &=& -\frac{1}{2}\iint \psi(x,y)\zeta(x,y)dxdy \nonumber \\
    &=& -\frac{1}{2}\iint\iint G(x,y,x',y')\zeta(x',y')\zeta(x,y)dx'dy'~dxdy,
\end{eqnarray}
where $G(x,y,x',y')$ is the Green's function that relates the streamfunction $\psi$ to the vorticity $\zeta$. 
The variation of the functional $S+\beta E$ leads to a ``$\sinh$'' relation between the vorticity and the streamfunction
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sinh_relation}
    \zeta = \alpha \sinh{\beta\psi},
\end{equation}
with the constants $\alpha$ for normalisation and $\beta$ for constraining the energy.
This relation has been verified via numerical simulations of 2D flat-bottom turbulence after long-time integrations \citep{montgomeryRelaxationTwoDimensions1992}.

When applying the Montgomery \& Joyce's theory to a QG system, previous authors approximated the PV field $q(x,y)$ by an ensemble of point vortices in three-dimensional baroclinic flows \citep{dibattistaEquilibriumStatisticalPredictions2001,funakoshiMaximumEntropyState2012}. 
Adopting a similar approach, we replace the relative vorticity $\zeta$ with the PV $q$ in the definition of the entropy (\ref{eq:entropy}):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:entropy_q}
    S(q_+, q_-) = -\iint q_+(x,y)\ln{q_+(x,y)}dxdy - \iint q_-(x,y)\ln{q_-(x,y)}dxdy, 
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}\label{eq:q_by_densities}
    q(x,y) = q_+(x,y) - q_-(x,y).
\end{equation}
The energy (\ref{eq:energy_by_vorticity}) for the QG system remains unchanged.
Then we arrive at a ``$\sinh$'' relation between the PV and the streamfunction similar to that in the flat-bottom case (\ref{eq:sinh_relation}), that is,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sinh_relation_qg}
    q = \alpha \sinh{\beta\psi}.
\end{equation}
Compared with the linear relation (\ref{eq:linear_relation}), the ``$\sinh$'' relation (\ref{eq:sinh_relation_qg}) is expected to yield strong vorticity outliers at large absolute values of streamfunction, which may be relevant to the isolated vortices observed in numerical simulations of 2D topographic turbulence \citep{siegelmanTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2023,heMultipleStatesTwodimensional2024}.

\subsection{Empirical theory}
\label{sec:empricical_theories}
As we will show in \S~\ref{sec:results}, neither the linear relation (\ref{eq:linear_relation}) of the minimum-enstrophy theory nor the ``$\sinh$'' relation (\ref{eq:sinh_relation_qg}) of the maximum-entropy theory alone can solely describe the final states of 2D topographic turbulence obtained from numerical simulations.
However, either relation can represent a fraction of the full solution.
We therefore aim to develop an empirical theory that incorporates both relations.
In the following, we first develop an empirical relation between the PV and the streamfunction, and then propose an empirical theoretical model for the final states of 2D topographic turbulence based on the two theories.

\subsubsection{Empirical relation between PV and streamfunction}
\label{sec:empirical_relation}
In previous numerical simulations \citep{siegelmanTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2023,heMultipleStatesTwodimensional2024}, the flow fields were dealt with through a decomposition between the background flow and isolated vortices.
\citet{siegelmanTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2023} and \citet{heMultipleStatesTwodimensional2024} paid attention to the background flow.
They found that, after removing vorticity outliers induced by isolated vortices from the simulation data, the remaining background flow approximately satisfies the linear relation (\ref{eq:linear_relation}), but with a linear slope $\mu$ below the value predicted by the minimum-enstrophy principle.
On the contrary, if we pay attention to the isolated vortices and remove the background flow from the simulation data, the remaining field might be described by the ``$\sinh$'' relation (\ref{eq:sinh_relation_qg}).
To verify this conjecture, it is necessary to remove the background flow appropriately, whose vorticity magnitude is typically much smaller than that of vortices.
Thus, the background flow cannot be removed from the PV field via a threshold.
However, it is observed that, with adequately strong isolated vortices stirring the background PV, the background flow approaches the PV-homogenised state with its streamfunction tending towards the topographic streamfunction (\ref{eq:topographic_streamfunction}).
By removing the topographic streamfunction from the total flow field, we conjecture an empirical relation described by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:empirical_relation}
    q \sim \alpha \sinh{\beta(\psi-\psi_\#)}.
\end{equation}
In \S~\ref{sec:results}, we will inspect the scatter plots of $q$ against both $\psi$ and $\psi-\psi_\#$ of the simulation data, to uncover their relations. 

\subsubsection{Empirical theoretical model of final states}
\label{sec:empirical_modelling}
Inspired by \citet{siegelmanTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2023}, we propose to theoretically model the final states of 2D topographic turbulence by a superposition of isolated vortices on the background flow, namely,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:model}
    f^m = f_b^m + f_v^m,
\end{equation}
with $f\in[q, \psi, u, v]$.
$f_b^m$ and $f_v^m$ are the models for the background flow and vortices, respectively.
In the following, we will develop the models for the background flow and vortices that are specific to the bump; adaptations to the zonal ridge will be discussed in the next section.

According to \citet{siegelmanTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2023} and \citet{heMultipleStatesTwodimensional2024}, upon the removal of vorticity outliers, the remaining background flow approximately satisfies a linear relation between its PV and streamfunction. 
We thus model the background flow as
\refstepcounter{equation}\label{eq:model_background_flow}
$$
q_b^m=\mu_m\psi_b^m; \quad q_b^m=\Delta\psi_b^m+\eta, \eqno{(\theequation{\mathit{a},\mathit{b}})}
$$
with $\mu_m$ as the linear slope for the modelled background flow.
For the bump (\ref{eq:bump}), $q_b^m$ is obtained as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bump_background_pv}
    q_b^m(x,y,\mu_m) = \frac{\mu_m}{\mu_m+1}(\cos{x} + \cos{y}).
\end{equation}
Other quantities, such as $\psi_b^m$, $u_b^m$ and $v_b^m$ can be readily derived.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{bump_qv-psiv.pdf}
    \caption{$q_v$--$\psi_v$ relation of the Gaussian profiles (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}) for $\Gamma_m=1.0$ and $L_m=0.5$.}
    \label{fig:bump_qv-psiv}
\end{figure}

On the other hand, upon successful verification of the relation (\ref{eq:empirical_relation}) that will be presented in \S~\ref{sec:results}, we can model the vortices by a ``$\sinh$'' relation between the corresponding PV and streamfunction, that is
\refstepcounter{equation}\label{eq:modeL_mortices}
$$
q_v^m = \alpha_m\sinh{\beta_m\psi_v^m}; \quad q_v^m=\Delta\psi_v^m,
\eqno{(\theequation{\mathit{a},\mathit{b}})}
$$
with some constants $\alpha_m$ and $\beta_m$.
In principle, the field of the vortices should be obtained by solving the ``$\sinh$''-Poisson equation.
However, in order to circumvent the difficulty of solving this nonlinear partial differential equation, we suppose that the vorticity of each vortex takes the Gaussian profile in physical space.
To be more specific, for the bump case (figure \ref{fig:topography}$a$) in which one cyclone and one anticyclone are expected to appear respectively at the centre (topographic maximum position) and corners (topographic minimum positions), $q_v^m$ is constructed as the superposition of the two Gaussian vortices,
\begin{subeqnarray}\label{eq:bump_vortices_pv}
    q_v^m(x,y,\Gamma_m,L_m) &=& \frac{\Gamma_m}{\pi L_m^2}\biggl\{
    \mathrm{exp}\left[-\frac{x^2+y^2}{L_m^2}\right] \\
    &-& \mathrm{exp}\left[-\frac{(x-\pi)^2+(y-\pi)^2}{L_m^2}\right] \\
    &-& \mathrm{exp}\left[-\frac{(x-\pi)^2+(y+\pi)^2}{L_m^2}\right] \\
    &-&\mathrm{exp}\left[-\frac{(x+\pi)^2+(y-\pi)^2}{L_m^2}\right] \\
    &-&\mathrm{exp}\left[-\frac{(x-\pi)^2+(y+\pi)^2}{L_m^2}\right]
    \biggl\},
\end{subeqnarray}
where (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}$a$) stands for a cyclone at the centre ($x=0,y=0$) and (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}$b$--$e$) stands for an anticyclone at the four corners ($x=\pm\pi, y=\pm\pi$).
The corresponding streamfunction $\psi_v^m$ can be obtained by applying the inverse Laplacian operator to $q_v^m$ in spectral space.
Although the model of vortices (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}) does not satisfy the ``$\sinh$'' relation exactly, the vorticity and the streamfunction of the Gaussian profiles (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}) closely adhere to the ``$\sinh$'' relation, as shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_qv-psiv}.
In the limit of $L_m\rightarrow 0$, the  Gaussians reduce to $\delta-$function distributions, which is consistent with the point-vortex model from which the ``$\sinh$'' relation was derived.
The Gaussian shape of vortices has been commonly found in numerical simulations of 2D free-decaying flat-bottom turbulence \citep{mcwilliamsVorticesTwodimensionalTurbulence1990,jimenezStructureVorticesFreely1996}.
It has also been used to model the vortex crystals on a polar $\gamma$-plane \citep{siegelmanPolarVortexCrystals2022}.

In the model of the vortices (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}), We have supposed that the two vortices take identical strength $\Gamma_m$ and length $L_m$, because the bump configuration with an identical magnitude of topographic maximum and minimum should accommodate a pair of antisymmetric vortices.
With this antisymmetry, the model of the vortices yields zero circulation, which is consistent with the prerequisite of the Montgomery \& Joyce's theory.





One should note that the superposition of vortices $q^m_v$ (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}) on the background flow $q^m_b$ (\ref{eq:bump_background_pv}) is not an exact solution to the stationary inviscid QG PV equation, that is, $J(\psi^m, q^m)\neq0$, although the background flow $q^m_b$ (\ref{eq:bump_background_pv}) or a single Gaussian vortex individually satisfies $J(\psi, q)=0$. 
There are interactions between different vortices and between vortices and the background flow.
However, if different vortices are well separated from each other, the interactions between different vortices can be weak and omitted; this is satisfied in our two configurations of topography (figure \ref{fig:topography}), since the topographic maximum is distant from the minimum.
On the other hand, the interaction between the background flow (\ref{eq:bump_background_pv}) and a Gaussian vortex sitting above a topographic extremum is negligible, because the background flow (\ref{eq:bump_background_pv}) around that extremum takes the asymptotic form $q_b^m\sim r^2$ ($r$ is the radial distance to the topographic extremum), whose contour lines parallel those of a Gaussian vortex.
To summarise, the superposition between the background flow $q^m_b$ (\ref{eq:bump_background_pv}) and the vortices $q^m_v$ (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}) can approximately satisfy the stationary inviscid QG PV equation locally around topographic extrema.

There are three free parameters in the model (\ref{eq:model}) with the background flow (\ref{eq:bump_background_pv}) and the vortices (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}), which are the linear slope $\mu_m$ of the background flow, the vortex strength $\Gamma_m$, and the vortex length $L_m$.
These parameters can be determined by noting that the integrals of the modelled state $f^m$ shall match those of the final state in a numerical simulation.
With three unknown parameters, we choose to constrain the energy, the total enstrophy, and the quartic Casimir of the modelled state $f^m$ by postulating
\refstepcounter{equation}\label{eq:model_consraint}
$$
E^m(\mu_m,\Gamma_m, L_m) = E, \quad Q^m(\mu_m,\Gamma_m, L_m) = Q, \quad C_4^m(\mu_m,\Gamma_m, L_m) = C_4.
\eqno{(\theequation{\mathit{a},\mathit{b},\mathit{c}})}
$$
% We do not consider the circulation as a constraint, since our model (\ref{eq:model},\ref{eq:bump_background_pv},\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}) corresponds to zero circulation, matching  automatically.
The terms on the left-hand side of the constraints (\ref{eq:model_consraint}) are calculated with the model $f^m$, and thus depend on the three unknown parameters $\mu_m$, $\Gamma_m$ and $L_m$; the terms on the right-hand side are diagnosed from the final states of the numerical simulations. 
By applying a nonlinear iterative solver to the constraints (\ref{eq:model_consraint}), the three parameters can be determined.

We note that the determination of theoretical model parameters by integrals of the flow states has also been adopted by the minimum-enstrophy and maximum-entropy theories, in which the Lagrange multipliers are determined by constraining the integral invariants.
However, our empirical model does not serve as a predictive tool, since only energy is nearly conserved in numerical simulations of 2D decaying turbulence. The total enstrophy $Q$ and the quartic Casimir $C_4$ drop drastically. How to theoretically infer the Casimirs at the end of simulations based on the initial conditions remains elusive.

\subsubsection{Adaptation to the zonal ridge}
\label{sec:adaptation_to_zonal_ridge}
The empirical theories developed in \S~\ref{sec:empirical_relation} and \S~\ref{sec:empirical_modelling} can be applied directly to the final states above the sinusoidal bump (figure \ref{fig:topography}$a$).
As we will show, the final states above the zonal ridge (figure \ref{fig:topography}$b$) are more complicated.
However, the empirical theories can be slightly modified and applied to the zonally averaged flow state, defined by
\begin{equation}
    \bar{f}(y,t) = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int^{+\pi}_{-\pi} f(x,y,t) dx.
\end{equation}
The empirical relation (\ref{eq:empirical_relation}) is then applied to the zonally averaged flow, that is,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:empirical_relation_zonal_flow}
    \bar{q} \sim \alpha \sinh{\beta(\bar{\psi}-\bar{\psi}_\#)}.
\end{equation}
The empirical superposition model (\ref{eq:model}) is also applied to the zonally averaged flow through a similar superposition between the background flow and vortices
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ridge_model}
    \bar{f}^m = \bar{f}_b^m + \bar{f}_v^m.
\end{equation}
The background flow after zonal average is still assumed to satisfy a linear relation between its PV and streamfunction, which yields the zonally averaged background PV for the zonal ridge (\ref{eq:ridge})
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ridge_model_background_pv}
    \bar{q}_b^m(y,\mu_m) = \frac{\mu_m}{\mu_m+1}\sqrt{2}\sin{y}.
\end{equation}
The zonally averaged vortices are one-dimensional and assumed to be a superposition of one-dimensional Gaussian profiles at topographic extrema $(y=\pm\pi/2)$, that is,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ridge_model_vortices_pv}
    \bar{q}_v^m(y, \Gamma_m, L_m) = \frac{\Gamma_m}{\sqrt{\pi} L_m}\biggl\{
    \mathrm{exp}\left[-\frac{(y-\pi/2)^2}{L_m^2}\right] - \mathrm{exp}\left[-\frac{(y+\pi/2)^2}{L_m^2}\right] \biggl\}.
\end{equation}
The three parameters in the modelled PV (\ref{eq:ridge_model_background_pv}) and (\ref{eq:ridge_model_vortices_pv}) are determined by matching the energy, the total enstrophy, and the quartic Casimir of the zonally averaged model 
with those diagnosed from the numerical simulations, namely,
\refstepcounter{equation}\label{eq:ridge_model_constraints}
$$
\bar{E}^m(\mu_m,\Gamma_m, L_m) = \bar{E}, \quad \bar{Q}^m(\mu_m,\Gamma_m, L_m) = \bar{Q}, \quad \bar{C}_4^m(\mu_m,\Gamma_m, L_m) = \bar{C}_4,
\eqno{(\theequation{\mathit{a},\mathit{b},\mathit{c}})}
$$
where the overbar denotes that the three integrals are calculated from the one-dimensional zonally averaged fields.
% Here, the left-hand terms are obtained by integration of the model $\bar{f}^m(y,\mu_m,\Gamma_m,L_m)=\bar{f}_b^m(y,\mu_m)+\bar{f}_v^m(y, \Gamma_m, L_m)$ (where $f=q, \psi$); the right-hand terms are obtained by integration of the zonally averaged fields derived from numerical simulations.
Then these model parameters are obtained by applying a nonlinear iterative solver to the constraints (\ref{eq:ridge_model_constraints}) as before.



\section{Numerical setup}
\label{sec:numerical_setup}
We numerically solve the QG PV equation using the open-source pseudo-spectral package \texttt{GeophysicalFlows.jl} running on GPUs \citep{constantinou2021geophysicalflows}.
The domain range is $[-\pi, \pi)\times [-\pi, \pi)$ with $L=2\pi$ and thus the smallest wavenumber of $1$.
The spatial resolution for all runs is $1024\times 1024$.
The dissipation term $D\zeta$ is implemented via a spectral filter of relative vorticity, which is applied to high wavenumbers at the end of each time step.
Time is stepped forward by a fourth-order Runge–Kutta scheme.
A time step of $0.002$ is chosen to ensure that the CFL number stays below $1$.
Simulations are forwarded in time for a large number of steps ($1.5\times 10^{7}$) to ensure that the decreasing rate of total enstrophy nearly plateaus and all vortex mergers almost complete, both indicating nearly-stationary final states.
These final states are then analysed and compared with the theories.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{bump_initial_zeta.pdf}
    \caption{Initial monoscale fields of the relative vorticity $\zeta$ centering around the wavenumbers $k_{ini}=[2,8,16,24,32,64]$ ($a$--$f$), but with the same energy level $0.25E_\#$.}
    \label{fig:initial_conditions}
\end{figure}

As in \citet{heMultipleStatesTwodimensional2024}, we utilise a series of initial conditions to obtain different final states of 2D free-decaying topographic turbulence.
We run a series of simulations initialised by structureless random fields of the relative vorticity $\zeta(x,y,0)$, centred around the wavenumber $k_{ini}$, with the bandwidth $[k_{ini}-0.5, k_{ini}+0.5]$.
These monoscale fields are characterised by the initial centroid wavenumber $k_{ini}$ and the energy level $E$.
The energy level is fixed at $E=0.25E_\#$ for all the simulations in this work.
We are particularly interested in the different final states reached by initial conditions with different total enstrophy but identical energy.
Different initial total enstrophy is obtained by varying the initial centroid wavenumber $k_{ini}$, thus the scale of the initial field.
We choose the scales of initial fields smaller than that of the domain-scale topography, corresponding to $k_{ini}>1$, since vortices at such scales are steered by the local topographic gradient and ultimately correlated positively with topographic extrema \citep{carnevalePropagationBarotropicVortices1991}.
The initial fields of relative vorticity with $k_{ini}=[2,8,16,24,32,64]$ are illustrated in figure \ref{fig:initial_conditions}. 
They are used for both topographic configurations.

Note that, since our initial fields are implemented in spectral space, the circulation of the initial relative vorticity is identically zero.
In addition, the equivalent circulations of the two topographies (\ref{eq:bump} and \ref{eq:ridge}) are zero.
Consequently, no total circulation is associated with the total PV, which is a prerequisite of the Montgomery \& Joyce's theory and also our empirical theory.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}
The results for the sinusoidal bump and zonal ridge are shown successively.
For each case, we first describe the phenomenology of the final states qualitatively.
Then, the relations between the PV and the streamfunction are investigated and compared with theories.
Finally, the empirical model is tested and compared against simulation data.

\subsection{Sinusoidal Bump}
\label{sec:results_bump}

\subsubsection{Phenomenology of final states}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{bump_contour.pdf}
    \caption{Final states of PV at $t=30000$ evolving from the initial fields (figure \ref{fig:initial_conditions}) above the sinusoidal bump (figure \ref{fig:topography}$a$).
    Dashed lines denote $\eta=0$ and separate the areas of $\eta>0$ (around the centre) and $\eta<0$ (around the corners).}
    \label{fig:bump_contour}
\end{figure}

The final states of PV at the time instant $t=30000$ resulting from different initial fields (figure \ref{fig:initial_conditions}) above the sinusoidal bump (figure \ref{fig:topography}$a$) are shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}.
The evolutions from these states for a short duration can be found in the supplementary movies. 
Despite long-time evolutions to reach these states, obvious unsteadiness remains.
In all cases, cyclones ($q>0$) cluster at the topographic maximum (centre of the domain)  and anticyclones ($q<0$) at the topographic minimum (corners of the domain).
However, these vortices differ for different initial wavenumbers, in terms of their unsteadiness, number, shape, size, and strength.
For the $k_{ini}=2$ case (figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}$a$), the resulting two vortices exhibit strong unsteadiness (see the corresponding movie): the edge of the cyclone deforms in time; the anticyclone is not fully topographically-locked but drifts around the topographic minimum.
For $k_{ini}\in[8, 16, 24, 32]$ (figures \ref{fig:bump_contour}$b$--$e$), both cyclones and anticyclones are nearly circular; they centre almost exactly above the topographic maximum and minimum, respectively.
The movies of these intermediate--$k_{ini}$ cases all show nearly stationary vortices.
We note that a tiny anticyclone persists around the cyclone for the $k_{ini}=32$ case (figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}$e$).
For the $k_{ini}=64$ case, multiple cyclones and anticyclones emerge as vortex crystals around the topographic maximum and minimum, respectively.
As shown in the corresponding movie, the cyclones are in solid-body rotation, while the anticyclones nearly freeze.
We note that the vortex crystals above either the topographic maximum or minimum resemble the polar vortices above a $\gamma$-plane as obtained by \citet{siegelmanPolarVortexCrystals2022}. 
This resemblance is understandable, because the topography (\ref{eq:bump}) around its extrema takes the asymptotic form of $\eta\sim r^2$ ($r$ is the radial distance to the extrema), hence imposing an analogous $\gamma$ effect to that over a polar cap.



We note that the final states are very sensitive to initial conditions.
With different initial random seeds, different final instantaneous states can arise.
However, the three regimes of vortices at low, intermediate, and high initial wavenumbers remain observable.





\subsubsection{PV-streamfunction relations}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{bump_q-psi.pdf}
    \caption{Scatter plots (in blue color) between $q$ and $\psi$ for different final states shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}. 
    Red lines show the linear relation with the slope $\mu=1$ predicted by the minimum-enstrophy theory at the energy $E=0.25E_\#$.}
    \label{fig:bump_pv-psi}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{bump_q-psi_fit.pdf}
    \caption{Scatter plots (in blue color) between $q$ and $\psi-\psi_\#$ for different final states shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}.
    Red lines show the least squares fitting between $q$ and $\alpha\sinh{\beta(\psi-\psi_\#)}$.}
    \label{fig:bump_pv-psi-psic}
\end{figure}

We then examine the PV-streamfunction relations of the final states and compare them with theories.
The PV-streamfunction scatter plots corresponding to the final states in figure \ref{fig:bump_contour} are shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_pv-psi}.
In general, these scatter plots reveal three main branches, delineated by two turning points at the maximum and minimum values of the streamfunction approximately.
The first branch, on the ``top'' of the scatter plots, shows positive PV and a decreasing trend of streamfunction with increasing PV. Based on geostrophic balance, this branch corresponds to an increasingly lower pressure anomaly, and thus the intense cyclone at the centre of the domain. 
The second branch, at the ``bottom'' of the scatter plots, shows negative PV but again a decreasing trend of streamfunction with increasing PV. Geostrophic balance then indicates an increasingly higher pressure anomaly and thus the strong anticyclone at the four corners of the domain.
The third branch, sandwiched in the ``middle'' of the scatter plots, is in the vicinity of the linear relation given by the minimum-enstrophy state (red lines in figure \ref{fig:bump_pv-psi}), and should correspond to areas away from the two vortices.
As $k_{ini}$ increases, the vortices become stronger, as indicated by the growing magnitudes of PV extrema and the broadened variations of streamfunction between the turning points and the PV extrema.
For the highest $k_{ini}=64$ case, the scatter plot is complicated by multiple branches, each of which shall correspond to a single cyclone or anticyclone shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}$(f)$.

The presence of turning points in the scatter plots discloses a non-monotonic variation of PV with streamfunction.
Such a non-monotonic behaviour cannot be described solely by the linear or the ``$\sinh$'' $q$--$\psi$ relation (\ref{eq:linear_relation} and \ref{eq:sinh_relation_qg}) based on the minimum-enstrophy or the maximum-entropy principle, respectively.
It is neither described by the maximum-entropy theory of Miller-Robert-Sommeria, since this theory also predicts a monotonic relation between voticity and streamfunction \citep{robertStatisticalEquilibriumStates1991,bouchetStatisticalMechanicsTwodimensional2012}.
% To our knowledge, it is first time that a non-monotonic relation between PV (or vorticity) and streamfunction is reported.



However, if the topographic streamfunction $\psi_\#$ (\ref{eq:topographic_streamfunction}) is removed from the total streamfunction, the scatter plots between $q$ and $\psi-\psi_\#$ are overall monotonic, as shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_pv-psi-psic}. Crucially, the scatter plots are ``$\sinh$''-like, and are suspected to satisfy the empirical relation (\ref{eq:empirical_relation}).
We perform least-squares fitting to determine $\alpha$ and $\beta$ in the empirical relation (\ref{eq:empirical_relation})  for each case; the results are shown in red color in figure \ref{fig:bump_pv-psi-psic}.
For the intermediate--$k_{ini}$ cases (figure \ref{fig:bump_pv-psi-psic}$b$--$e$), where only two strong vortices exist in a single domain, the closeness between data and the fitted curves is good, although the PV can increase super-exponentially near the extrema of $\psi-\psi_\#$ (see figures \ref{fig:bump_pv-psi-psic}$c,d$).
For the $k_{ini}=2$ case (figure \ref{fig:bump_pv-psi-psic}$a$), the imprint of the ``$\sinh$'' relation remains visible; the poor closeness may result from continuously deforming and strolling vortices around topographic extrema.
For the $k_{ini}=64$ case (figure \ref{fig:bump_pv-psi-psic}$f$), the presence of multiple branches of vortices makes it impossible to frame the flow with a simple ``$\sinh$'' relation, which hosts only two branches.
Yet even in this case, like-signed vortices cluster towards the same end of the remaining streamfunction $\psi-\psi_\#$.
In conclusion, upon the removal of the topographic streamfunction $\psi_\#$, the general trend of the ``$\sinh$'' relation is observed in the scatter plots between $q$ and $\psi-\psi_\#$; very good closeness between the data and fitted curves is observed when there are only two strong vortices in a single domain.




\subsubsection{Theoretical model}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{bump_modelling_ki=8.pdf}
    \caption{Comparisons of the PV ($a$), streamfunction ($b$), zonal velocity ($c$), and meridional velocity ($d$) along the dash dotted line shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}$a$ between simulation data and theoretical model for the final state corresponding to $k_{ini}=8$ (figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}$b$).
    $q$ in $(a)$ is shown in symlog scale in order to visualize the data within the range $[-1,1]$.}
    \label{fig:bump_modelling_kini=8}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{bump_modelling_ki=32.pdf}
    \caption{Same as figure \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=8} but for the final state corresponding to $k_{ini}=32$ (figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}$e$).}
    \label{fig:bump_modelling_kini=32}
\end{figure}

Upon successful validation of the empirical relation (\ref{eq:empirical_relation}), we assess the performance of the superposition model (\ref{eq:model}) together with the background model  (\ref{eq:bump_background_pv}) and the vortex model (\ref{eq:bump_vortices_pv}) in reconstructing the final states.
We mainly test the model on the intermediate--$k_{ini}$ ($8, 16, 24, 32$) cases (see figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}$b$--$e$) where there are only one cyclone and one anticyclone that are axisymmetric and fully locked to the respective topographic extremum.
In figures \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=8} and \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=32}, we compare the final states in numerical simulations with those reconstructed through our model for the $k_{ini}=8$ and $32$ cases, respectively.
We show the profiles of PV $q$, streamfunction $\psi$, zonal velocity $u$, and meridional velocity $v$ on the diagonal line of the domain (see the dash dotted line in  figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}$a$).
The results of the $k_{ini}=16$ and $24$ are similar and not shown.



As shown in figures \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=8}$a$ and \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=32}$a$ for comparisons of PV, the vortex model $q^m_v$ (in green color) can capture the peak and valley; the background model $q^m_b$ (in blue color) can capture the transition zone in between; the superposition of $q^m_v$ on $q^m_b$ (in red color) can capture the overal spatial variations of PV.
There are smaller-scale structures of PV that the background model $q^m_b$ fails to capture, such as the PV staircases outside of the peak and valley as shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=8}$a$.
As vortices become stronger, the PV staircases are weakened and the background model shows improved performance (see figure \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=32}$a$).
Despite the failure of the theoretical model to depict smaller-scale PV structures, the superposition model is adequate to capture the large-scale fields of the streamfunction and velocity components, as shown in figures \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=8}$b$--$d$ and \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=32}$b$--$d$.
One should note that the flow direction of the background field is opposite to that of vortices, as illustrated by the streamfunction and velocity components in figures \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=8}$b$--$d$ and \ref{fig:bump_modelling_kini=32}$b$--$d$, consistent with distinct correlations of the background flow and vortices with topography.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{bump_modelling_parameters.pdf}
    \caption{Model parameters $\mu_m$, $\Gamma_m$, and $L_m$ for different $k_{ini}$. The topography is the sinusoidal bump.}
    \label{fig:bump_model_parameters}
\end{figure}

In figure \ref{fig:bump_model_parameters}, we show the model parameters for different $k_{ini}$ cases.
For the background model, the linear slope $\mu_m$ is smaller than $1$, which is the value for the minimum-enstrophy solution at the energy level of our simulations.
This is consistent with former observations of background flows upon the removal of vorticity outliers \citep{siegelmanTwodimensionalTurbulenceTopography2023,heMultipleStatesTwodimensional2024}; the vortices mix the background PV towards homogenization and thus lower the linear slope of the background flow.
However, the increase of $\mu_m$ with $k_{ini}$ in the range of $[8,32]$ is inconsistent with previous results above a random topography \citep{heMultipleStatesTwodimensional2024}; higher $k_{ini}$ is expected to provide more seeds for vortices to enhance PV mixing and thus to lower the linear slope $\mu_m$.
Despite that, the highest $k_{ini}=64$ case with the presence of multiple vortices shows a more homogenized background PV, as compared to the $k_{ini}=8$--$32$ cases (see figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}); this is consistent with the result above a random topography \citep{heMultipleStatesTwodimensional2024}.
We suspect that, for the $k_{ini}=8$--$32$ cases, there exist only two vortices in a single domain, which may not be strong enough for mixing the PV towards homogenization.
% Actually, the variation of $\mu_m$ in the $k_{ini}\in[8,32]$ as shown in figure \ref{fig:bump_model_parameters}$(a)$ is not  significant, and might be affected by numerical errors when solving the nonlinear equations for constraints.
On the other hand, as $k_{ini}$ increases, the vortex strength $\Gamma_m$ generally increases (figure \ref{fig:bump_model_parameters}$b$) and the the vortex length $L_m$ decreases (figure \ref{fig:bump_model_parameters}$c$), which means that the vortices become stronger.



\subsection{Zonal ridge}
\label{sec:result_ridge}

\subsubsection{Phenomenology of final states}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{ridge_pv-contour.pdf}
    \caption{Final states of PV at $t=30000$ evolving from the initial fields (figure \ref{fig:initial_conditions}) above the zonal ridge (figure \ref{fig:topography}$b$).}
    \label{fig:ridge_contour}
\end{figure}

The final states of PV above the zonal ridge (figure \ref{fig:topography}$b$) at the time instant $t=30000$  are shown in figure \ref{fig:ridge_contour}.
The time evolutions starting from these states for a short duration can be found in the supplementary movies.

For the $k_{ini}=2$ case, no vortices emerge.
There are meandering zero PV contours near $y=\pm\pi$ and $y=0$.
The corresponding movie shows strong unsteadiness, as the zero PV contours at $y=\pm\pi$ and $y=0$ propagate in opposite zonal directions.
The solution may have reflected topographic Rossby waves with ``local westward'' phase propagation defined relative to the topographic PV gradient \citep{carnevalePropagationBarotropicVortices1991}.
Since our focus is on vortices, we refrain from quantifying the topographic Rossby waves in this work.

% since the gradient of the topographic PV (\ref{eq:ridge}) at the positions ($y=\pm\pi$ and $y=0$) attains the strongest value.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{ridge_zonal-average_pv.pdf}
    \caption{Time series of zonally averaged PV above the zonal ridge through the time interval of $[30000, 30200]$.}
    \label{fig:ridge_zonal-average_pv}
\end{figure}

For the $k_{ini}=8$--$64$ cases, the most striking feature of the final states above the zonal ridge is the emergence of one cyclonic vortex array and one anticyclonic array aligned with the topographic maximum ($y=\pi/2$) and minimum ($y=-\pi/2$), respectively, contrasting with typically a single vortex aloft each topographic extremum in the bump case (figure \ref{fig:bump_contour}).
This contrast is understandable, since the topographic extrema of the zonal ridge and bump are lines and points, respectively.
Multiple vortices can emerge as an array in the homogeneous direction of the zonal ridge.
Within each vortex array, vortices almost cease to merge, constituting vortex crystals.
As shown by the corresponding movies, all vortex arrays propagate zonally.
However, the propagation directions of vortex arrays with different $k_{ini}$ and polarities are rather random. 
The vortices at low-$k_{ini}$ cases ($k_{ini}=8, 16$) are more oval-shaped with major axes along the $y$ direction, contrasting with the axisymmetric vortex structures in high--$k_{ini}$ cases ($k_{ini}=24, 32, 64$).

We note that the vortex arrays aligned in the homogeneous direction of topography are analogous to the ``String of Pearls'' observed in the atmosphere of Jupiter \citep{hill2017jupiter}.
Our simulations shed light on their formation mechanism by suggesting that the environmental PV might reach a local extremum, staying homogeneous in the direction of the string and inhomogeneous in the perpendicular direction.

The time series of the zonally averaged PV $\bar{q}$ are shown in figure \ref{fig:ridge_zonal-average_pv}.
The $k_{ini}=2$ case displays weak time-periodicity roughly at $y=\pm\pi$ above a quasi-stationary background flow, which may link to a periodic-orbit solution.
For the $k_{ini}=8$--$64$ cases, the solutions display two major bands with elevated PV magnitudes around the topographic extrema $y=\pm\pi/2$ above a weaker quasi-stationary background flow.
These bands result from the zonal averaging of vortex arrays shown in figure \ref{fig:ridge_contour}, and can be considered as one-dimensional vortices.
For the $k_{ini}=64$ case, there are some secondary bands caused by neighbouring smaller vortices.

In contrast to the solutions in two dimensions, the zonally averaged solutions above the zonal ridge present a flow configuration with two vortices above a background flow, which is similar to that over the bump.
Thus, the empirical relation and theoretical model presented in \S~\ref{sec:adaptation_to_zonal_ridge} can be adapted and applied to the zonal ridge as follows.

\subsubsection{PV-streamfunction relations}
In figure \ref{fig:ridge_q-psi}, we show the scatter plots between the zonally averaged $\bar{q}$ and $\bar{\psi}-\bar{\psi}_\#$ in blue.
Simulation results through the time interval $30000$ to $30200$ incremented by $2$ are displayed.
In the wave-dominated regime with $k_{ini}=2$, the scatter plots at different time instants do not overlap, because of strong unsteadiness of the solution.
In the vortex-dominated regime with $k_{ini}=8$--$64$, the overlapping hints at a nearly stationary solution that corresponds to a converged PV-streamfunction relation.
Furthermore, the scatter plots between the zonally averaged $\bar{q}$ and $\bar{\psi}-\bar{\psi}_\#$ in the vortex-dominated regime ($k_{ini}=8$--$64$) simply display two main branches that correspond to the positive and negative one-dimensional vortices in the band form shown in figure \ref{fig:ridge_zonal-average_pv}.
However, different from the results above the bump (figure \ref{fig:bump_pv-psi-psic}), there exist stronger PV staircases outside the vortices, especially for the low $k_{ini}=8$ case (see figure \ref{fig:ridge_q-psi}$b$).
We performed least-squares fitting between $\bar{q}$ and $\bar{\psi}-\bar{\psi}_\#$ towards the ``$\sinh$'' relation, as shown in red color in figure \ref{fig:ridge_q-psi}.
In general, the relations between $\bar{q}$ and $\bar{\psi}-\bar{\psi}_\#$ are close to the ``$\sinh$'' relation for the vortex-dominated cases ($k_{ini}=8$--$64$), despite that the latter cannot capture the PV staircases outside the vortices.
However, as $k_{ini}$ becomes larger, vortices become stronger and dominate over the PV staircases, the discrepancy between the data and the  ``$\sinh$'' relation becomes smaller.
