\subsection{Flow rate and mass conservation} \label{Sec:flowrate}
Flow rate, $q$, arises when two approaching spheres squeeze the fluid trapped between them, and is given by the relative movement of the particles, the dimensionless flow rate, $Q$, renders as follows:

\begin{equation}
Q = \frac{q}{\pi a^2 V} = 2 \int_{Z_1(R)}^{Z_2(R)} u(R,Z) R \; dZ.
\label{Eq:flow1}
\end{equation}
\	
The expression (\ref{Eq:flow1}) indicates that the flow-rate is a function of the radial direction, therefore, the driving-force that drives the velocity profile (pressure gradient in this case) must have a determined value in order to satisfy the mass conservation. Integrating the mass conservation equation  \citep{HoriBOOK2006,RuangStonePRF2024} in (\ref{Eq:Cont_Adim}) under the boundary condition in (\ref{Eq:CF}), in
proper dimensionless form, i.e. $w(Z_1) = 0$ and $w(Z_2) = -1$, it can be written as:

\begin{equation}
\int_{Z_1(R)}^{Z_2(R)} u R \; dZ - \frac{R^2}{2} = 0.
\label{Eq:Cont_int}	
\end{equation}
\
Multiplying (\ref{Eq:Cont_int}) by $2$, it may be re-cast as:
\begin{equation}
2\int_{Z_1(R)}^{Z_2(R)} u R \; dZ - R^2 = Q - R^2 = 0.
\label{Eq:Cont_mas}
\end{equation}
\
Then, (\ref{Eq:Cont_mas}) establishes the mass-conservation condition that will be employed for subsequent calculations (see \ref{appB} for further details). The steps to determine velocity profile, pressure gradient and solution of (\ref{Eq:Cont_mas}) at each radial position, from the centreline ($R_i=0$) to the edge of the spheres ($R_i=a$), assuming spheres of the same radius $\left( \kappa_a = \frac{1}{2} \right)$, are given as follows:

\begin{itemize}
\item First, for a given radial position ($R_i$), two estimates of $\frac{\partial P}{\partial R}_i$ are proposed from the Newtonian solution: $\frac{\partial P}{\partial R}_{New} = \frac{-6R}{H^3(R)}$, which is equal to the expression given in \cite{VazquezElleroPF2016} in dimensionless form. 
\
\item Secondly, $Q$ is calculated for both estimates. Here, the integral on the right-hand-side of (\ref{Eq:flow1}) is determined by applying the Simpson's 3/8 rule.
\
\item Finally, the secant method is used to determine a new estimate for $\frac{\partial P}{\partial R}_i$. This method is employed until $|Q\left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial R}_i \right) - R_i^2 | < 10^{-6}$.
\end{itemize}

The pressure gradient profile in the radial direction is illustrated in figure \ref{Fig:pressureDe}(a). The pressure gradient shows a maximum near the centreline, beyond which it drops steeply along radial direction. The pressure gradient increases slightly for $De = \{10^{-3}, 10^{-2}\}$; however, for $De = 10^{-1}$ it can be up to $\approx 4\%$ higher than in the Newtonian case at the peak.
\
Then, the pressure can be approximated by integrating the pressure gradient from the figure \ref{Fig:pressureDe}(a) within integration limits ranging from $R=0$ to $R = a$ using the Euler's method, to calculate the expression:

\begin{equation}
P = - \int_{R}^{\infty} \frac{\partial P}{\partial R} \; d R \approx - \int_{R}^{a} \frac{\partial P}{\partial R} \; d R.
\label{Eq:Pressure}
\end{equation}
\
Pressure attains its maximum in the centreline, located at $R=0$, and declines rapidly within a small radius range, as illustrated in figure \ref{Fig:pressureDe}(b). Solutions under small \textit{De}-numbers $De=\{10^{-3},10^{-2}\}$ seem similar to the Newtonian-solution. Only under $De = 10^{-1}$, there is an evident deviation from the Newtonian behaviour, $\approx 6\%$ higher than in the Newtonian case, at the peak.
These findings may be explained from an analysis of (\ref{Eq:Mom_rAdim_s}) and (\ref{Eq:Cont_mas}), where one may find an additional force from the viscoelastic characteristics of the fluid, resulting from the first normal stress-difference. This is indicated by a slight reduction in velocity profile (figure \ref{Fig:velocityDe}), which consequently leads to a decrease in flow rate as described in (\ref{Eq:flow1}). Therefore, to maintain mass conservation detailed in (\ref{Eq:Cont_mas}), there is a corresponding rise in both the pressure gradient and pressure.

