\subsection{Stress and normal forces}

The normal force acting on the particles' surface, $f$, is therefore obtained by integration of the total stresses along their surface. Again, Euler's method is employed to calculate the force from the numerical approximation of pressure in the range of $R=0$ to $R = a$. The dimensionless net viscoelastic lubricating force normal, $F$, to the approach velocity direction on the particle surface $S_2$ is given by:

\begin{equation}
F = \frac{f}{6\pi \eta_0 V a}= \frac{1}{3 \epsilon} \int_0^{\infty} P\mid_{Z = Z_2(R)} R \; d R \approx  \frac{1}{3 \epsilon} \int_0^{a} P\mid_{Z = Z_2(R)} R \; d R.
\label{Eq:force_exp}
\end{equation}
\
Figure \ref{Fig:ForceDeNew} compares the percentage increase of viscoelastic lubrication force to that of Newtonian lubrication force, given in dimensionless form such as: $F_{Newt} = \frac{1}{4 \epsilon}$. As illustrated in figure \ref{Fig:ForceDeNew}, the lubrication force exerted on the particle surface $S_2$ increases weakly due to increases in the pressure gradient caused by the viscoelastic response. Notably, the increasing presence of the Newtonian solvent in the dissolution, measured under a $\beta$-increase, reduces the force intensity. For $De = 1 \times 10^{-1}$ and $\beta = 0$, the viscoelastic lubrication force shows the greatest rise in relation to the Newtonian lubrication force, reaching an increase of up to $\approx 2 \%$. In a steady state, the lubrication force exerted on the particles is immediate. As discussed in this section, the incorporation of viscoelasticity into the momentum balance equation (via $N_1$) results in minor effects in the velocity, pressure gradient, and pressure distribution. Thus, the effect of viscoelasticity on the lubrication force is small when the fluid response is considered instantaneous and when the effects of geometric changes derived from the particle approach are not considered.

