The computations for pressure gradient, pressure, and lubrication force follow the same numerical techniques outlined in Section \ref{Sec:flowrate} adding the functionality with time through the terms $Z_2(R,T)$, $g(T)$ and $f(T)$. Despite the steady-state problem, given the time in the domain $(0 \leq T < 1)$, the viscoelastic lubrication force is expected to increase over time or increase as distance is reduced, according to (\ref{Eq:HRT}). In order to detect a significant alteration in distance, time needs to exceed $0.9$, such as $T > 0.9$.

We compare our solutions with expressions (52) and (54) in \cite{ZhengetalPF2023}, corresponding to the pressure and the lubrication force, respectively. The principal distinction between our formulation and that of \cite{ZhengetalPF2023} lies in their use of a Newtonian velocity profile and a stress-based boundary condition to compute the pressure gradient. Figure \ref{Fig:press_trans} presents the time evolution of the pressure for different values of \textit{De}-numbers. For early times, specially when $T \lesssim 0.3$, our predictions yields pressures lower than those of the Newtonian reference solution. However, as $T \gtrsim 0.3$, the viscoelastic predictions converge to, and marginally exceed, the Newtonian result. In that sense, the development of the viscoelastic time-dependent functions in (\ref{Eq:ODEu}) rise both pressure and lubrication force. A notable divergence from (52) becomes apparent as $T \rightarrow 1$. Their model predicts a decline in pressure, approaching negative values, in contrast to the monotonic increase characteristic of the Newtonian solution.
\
For the lubrication force in figure \ref{Fig:forceDe_Zheng}, our solutions are in close agreement with the analytical predictions of \cite{ZhengetalPF2023} at short times. Also, both predictions continue in close agreement with the Newtonian upper limit for all selected values of \textit{De}-numbers. Nonetheless, for $T \gtrsim 0.2$, Zheng's predictions indicate a lubrication force that exceeds our predicted values.

