\section{Model predictions} \label{Sec:ModelPre}

While comparing with \cite{ZhengetalPF2023} provides insights into the behaviour of pressure and lubrication forces over time, it is typical to represent the lubrication force relative to the distance between the spheres' contours. This can be achieved by evaluating (\ref{Eq:HRT}) at various time intervals. In figure \ref{Fig:forceDe_H0}, results for a Newtonian fluid and for different viscoelastic fluids described by Oldroyd-B model are illustrated. Also, we compare our solutions to the predictions for a sphere moving towards a rigid plane in \cite{RuangStonePRF2024} for $De = \{ 5 \times 10^{-3}, 5 \times 10^{-2} \}$. Ruangkriengsin's predictions consider a Newtonian expression plus extra terms coming from the first-order and second-order corrections of the . The Newtonian fluid displays a linear trend, on logarithmic scales, along all values of $H_0$, whereas for the Oldroyd-B model a linear trend is exhibited only for values of $H_0$ between $\approx (0.1 - 0.6)$. In contrast, at $H_0 = (0.6 - 1)$, the viscoelastic lubrication force begins at zero and increases until the Newtonian limit it reached. For shorter distances ($H_0 \lesssim 0.1$), however, it surpasses the Newtonian lubrication force and continues increasing.
