The enhancement of the viscoelastic lubrication force compared to the Newtonian lubrication force increases as distance reduces, as is detailed in table \ref{Tb:Flub}. Notably, even at the smallest \textit{De}-number ($5 \times 10^{-3}$), this increase can be as high as $70\%$, whilst at the higher \textit{De}-number ($1 \times 10^{-1}$), it can be up to two orders of magnitude greater than the Newtonian lubrication force. This considerable rise in lubrication force for viscoelastic fluids under transient dynamics, characterized by the Oldroyd-B model, could be crucial for modelling concentrated/dense systems with particles suspended in a viscoelastic medium with constant shear viscosity (Boger fluids). It is crucial to bear in mind that we have considered a constant \textit{De}-number. However, as the spheres continue to close in, this gap changes and so does \textit{De}, see (\ref{Eq:adimvar}), where \textit{De} is expected to increase as the interparticle decreases, possibly further enhancing this effect.

