\subsection{Effect of the first normal stress-difference} \label{Sec:NormalS}

To clarify the increase in the viscoelastic lubrication force using the Oldroyd-B model, we examined the topology of the velocity gradient of the interface separating both spheres. As outlined in Section \ref{Sec:Steady}, the viscoelastic response in the momentum balance is represented by the first normal stress-difference, such as: $\alpha(T) \left( \frac{\partial u}{\partial Z} \right)^2$, see (\ref{Eq:ODEu}), which denotes an extra resistance to shearing deformation of the fluid during the constant velocity approaching. Subsequently, this lubrication force enhancement may emerge by this extra term due to viscoelasticity, which scales with the square of the shear-rate. In addition, the major contribution to this resistance occurs near the centre of the particle gap.
Figure \ref{Fig:dvrdz} illustrates the velocity gradient magnitude in the interparticle separation between the spheres over time, where the blue colour represents low magnitude and red colour signifies high magnitude. When $H_0 = 0.49$, the velocity gradient across the space between particles remains minimal. At $H_0 = 0.10$, there is a slight increase in the velocity gradient magnitude on the surface of the spheres near the centre with magnitude around $\sim 1$, which leads to a minor rise in the lubrication force. As the distance decreases further, at $H_0 = 0.02$, the velocity gradient magnitude rises moderately to a pick value of $\sim 30$. Moreover, at distance $H_0 = 0.001$, the velocity gradient magnitude increase abruptly up to $\sim 3000$. Given that the effect of the viscoelasticity is proportional to the square of the velocity gradient magnitude, even with small values of \textit{De}, the velocity gradient magnitude at $H_0 = \{0.02, 0.001 \}$ is large enough to significantly enhance fluid resistance to be squeezed under constant velocity. This, in turn, increases the pressure gradient needed to sustain mass conservation. The result is a huge enhancement in the lubrication force near contact.

