\subsection{Boger fluid problem}

We consider the rheology data for the S1 fluid (a dissolution of polyacrylamide in corn syrup with composition: $0.05\%$ Separan AP 30, $2.4\%$ $H_2O$, and $97.55\%$ Corn Syrup 41N) in \cite{PhanetalJNNM1985}, which may be described by Oldroyd-B model, in which the mechanics of a thin film being squeezed between two parallel plates is studied, in contrast to our current analysis, where two approaching spheres are considered. The flow properties of this Boger fluid are $\eta_0 = 55.5 \; Pa \cdot s$, $\eta_s = 15.8 \; Pa \cdot s$ and $\lambda = 5 \; s$. In addition and for back reference, two Newtonian fluids are considered under $\eta_0 = \{15.8, 55.5\} \; Pa \cdot s$.
The transient forces are calculated by evaluating (\ref{Eq:force_exp}) on the particle $S_2$ at different times under constant squeezing velocity. Moreover, we investigate the viscoelastic force when the spheres move away from each other. 
The approaching case starts from $h_0 = 2.5 \times 10^{-4}$ to $h_0 = 2.5 \times 10^{-7}$, meanwhile the moving away instance starts from $h_0 = 2.5 \times 10^{-7}$ to $h_0 = 2.5 \times 10^{-4}$, both cases kicking off from rest.

Figure \ref{Fig:forceDe} shows how the viscoelastic force behaves with varying \textit{De}-numbers over time when utilizing the fluid S1 \citep{PhanetalJNNM1985}. As the spheres approach each other (illustrated in figure \ref{Fig:forceDe}(a) as: solid lines for Newtonian fluids and filled symbols for Boger materials), the force increases as time progresses, whereas when the spheres separate (in figure \ref{Fig:forceDe}(b) illustrated as: solid lines for Newtonian fluids and blank symbols for Boger fluids) the force decreases with time. For both Newtonian fluids, the lubrication force observed at the end of the approaching case match with the initial lubrication force noted during the separation scenario, and vice versa. Contrastingly, with the Boger fluids the lubrication force at the end of the approaching case is larger than the initial lubrication force at the beginning of the separation scenario. Consistently, the starting viscoelastic lubrication force during the separation starts at a lower level with respect to the end of the approaching case.
During the sphere approach and separation modes, the viscoelastic lubrication force seems constrained by a lower limit corresponding to the Newtonian fluid of viscosity $\eta_0 = \eta_s = 15.8 \; Pa \cdot s$, and an upper limit which corresponds to the Newtonian fluid of viscosity $\eta_0 = \eta_s + \eta_p = 55.5 \; Pa \cdot s$. In the sphere approaching case, for $0.4 \lesssim T < 1$, the viscoelastic force exceeds the Newtonian reference, as predicted in \cite{PhanetalJNNM1985}.

