\section{Conclusions} \label{Sec:Conclusions}
In this work, the dynamics of the squeezing flow between two spheres embedded in a viscoelastic fluid, which may be described by an Oldroyd-B model, was examined. By focusing on the scenario of very narrow gaps between spheres, their shapes can be approximated as paraboloids. This simplification permits the use of lubrication theory to solve the mass and momentum balance equations employing an order-of-magnitude analysis. We consider weak viscoelasticity through small \textit{De}-numbers. Differently to other approaches, we solved for the velocity profile analytically and employed numerical methods to calculate the pressure and normal force. 
\
Once the squeeze flow is established, the momentum balance is composed by a viscous response coupled with a viscoelastic response leading by the first normal-stress differences (from the polymeric viscoelastic stress). Such momentum balance formulation reproduces a distinct velocity profile from the equivalently-viscous Newtonian profile, fact that evidences the viscoelastic contribution to the flow dynamics.
\
In the case of steady-state conditions, representing an immediate system response, viscoelasticity has a marginal effect in the lubrication force due to the small \textit{De}-numbers imposed up to $\sim 2\%$. The lubrication force displays a linear increase with the \textit{De}-number and $\beta$-fraction.
\
Conversely, for the transient solutions, changes of the gap lead to a time-dependent evolution of normal-stress difference that enhance resistance to flow by up to two orders of magnitude compared to the Newtonian response. This resistance is manifested through larger pressure gradients that work as the driven-force that ejects the fluid trapped between the spheres, even at the relatively small \textit{De}-numbers imposed. Furthermore, viscoelasticity induces non-symmetric behaviour when spheres approach or separate. These phenomena arising from viscoelasticity may be utilised to modelling the rheological behaviour of dense suspension in viscoelastic Boger fluids, dominated by short-range interactions \citep{KumaretalJNNFM2020,RuizLopezetalJRheol2023tribo,RosalesetalJNNFM2024}.


