\begin{rem}\label{rem:t=t_c}
In the case $t=t_c$, we have $P=0$. Since we have shown $t_c<-1$, the conditions for Theorem
\ref{thm:IY}\eqref{it:Gibbs} still hold, giving a Gibbs equilibrium measure $\mu_\Omega$ on
$\Omega$. Since $P=0$,
the cylinder sets have measure $\mu_\Omega([(m,n)])\approx(mn)^{t_c}$. 
The finiteness of the entropy also holds (again using $t_c<-1$) so this is the unique
equilibrium measure $\mu_\Omega$ on $\Omega$ for the potential $\Psi_{\Omega,0}=t_c\Phi_{\A_\Omega}$.
One can see that the expected 
return time is finite if and only if $t_c<-2$. Although we gave upper and lower 
bounds for $t_c$, we were not able to decide whether $t_c\ge -2$ or $t_c<-2$. 
Accordingly, we state a conditional result. 

If it is the case that $t_c<-2$, $\mu_\Omega$ 
gives rise to a corresponding measure on $\Sigma$ which we call $\mu$. We check, as before,
that $h_\mu(T)+t_c\chi(\mu,\A)=0$, so that $\mu$ is the third equilibrium measure. In this case,
for $t>t_c$, we have $P(t\Phi_{\A})\geq h_{\mu}(T)+t\chi(\mu, \A)= (t-t_c)\chi(\mu, \A)$.
Since $\chi(\mu,\A)>0$ by Corollary \ref{positive LE}, this establishes that
the right derivative of $P(t\Phi_{\A})$ is positive, showing that the phase transition
is first order.

If $t_c\ge -2$, the equilibrium state on $\Omega$ does not lift to an equilibrium measure
on $\Sigma$ and the only equilibrium measures for $t_c\Phi_\A$ are $\delta_{\bar 1}$ and 
$\delta_{\bar 2}$.
\end{rem}
