\begin{abstract}
High-order harmonic generation (HHG) enables the up-conversion of intense infrared or visible femtosecond laser pulses into extreme-ultraviolet attosecond pulses.
However, the highly nonlinear nature of the process results in low conversion efficiency, which can be a limitation for applications requiring substantial pulse energy, such as nonlinear attosecond time-resolved spectroscopy or single-shot diffractive imaging.
%As a consequence, high pulse energy and often low repetition rate driving lasers are used in bulky attosecond beam lines.
Refocusing of the attosecond pulses is also essential to achieve a high intensity, but difficult in practice due to strong chromatic aberrations.
%often prevent proper refocusing of all harmonics to the same longitudinal position.
In this work, we address both the generation and the refocusing of attosecond pulses by sculpting the driving beam into a ring-shaped intensity profile with no spatial phase variations, referred to as a Hollow Gaussian beam (HGB).
Our experimental and theoretical results reveal that HGBs efficiently redistribute the driving laser energy in the focus, where the harmonics are generated on a ring with low divergence, which furthermore decreases with increasing order.
\textcolor{black}{Although generated as a ring,} the attosecond pulses can be refocused with greatly reduced chromatic spread, therefore reaching higher intensity. %significantly brighter refocused HHG emission across the entire spectrum.
\textcolor{black}{This approach enhances the intensity of refocused attosecond pulses and enables significantly higher energy to be delivered in the driving beam without altering the focusing conditions. These combined advantages open pathways for compact, powerful, tabletop, laser-driven attosecond light sources.}
%High-order harmonic generation (HHG) enables the up-conversion of intense infrared/visible femtosecond laser pulses into extreme-ultraviolet attosecond pulses. However, its low conversion efficiency---inherent to its highly nonlinear nature---combined with the limited performance of refocusing optics in this high-frequency regime, constraints its practical use in applications such as x-ray diffraction or nonlinear x-ray spectroscopy. Nevertheless, the intrinsic coherence of HHG allows for precise spatial control of high-harmonic wavefronts through tailored shaping of the driving beam. Previous studies have shown that wavefront shaping can improve the focusing conditions of specific harmonic orders by compensating dipole phase distortions. However, since this effect is harmonic-dependent, such approaches fail to enable bright focusing of attosecond pulses. In this work, we demonstrate that the focusing conditions of \emph{all} harmonics can be simultaneously enhanced by sculpting the driving beam into a ring-shaped intensity profile with no spatial phase variations---a Hollow Gaussian beam (HGB). Our experimental and theoretical results reveal that HGBs efficiently redistribute the driving laser energy, leading to harmonic divergence decreasing with increasing harmonic order, and significantly brighter refocused HHG emission across the entire spectrum. This approach enables the enhancement of attosecond pulse brightness---up to a factor of three compared to Gaussian driving beams under optimal conditions---paving the way for powerful tabletop laser-driven attosecond light sources.
\end{abstract}

