\end{align}
then we compute the bivariate weighted varextropy as 
\begin{align}
VJ^w(X, Y)=\frac{1}{2916\theta^2}\left(3e^{\frac{3}{\theta}}E_1\left(\frac{3}{\theta}\right)(\theta^2-3\theta-9)+\theta^2+9\theta\right)
-\frac{1}{4096\theta^2}
\left(2e^{\frac{2}{\theta}}E_1\left(\frac{2}{\theta}\right)(\theta+2)-\theta\right)^{2},
\end{align}
where $E_1(\frac{i}{\theta})=\int_{i}^{+\infty}\frac{e^{-\frac{x}{\theta}}}{x}dx$ for $i=2, 3$.
It is necessary to mention, if $\theta=0$ or $X$ and $Y$ are independent and have standard exponential distribution then 
$VJ^w(X, Y)=\frac{295}{2985984}.$
