\section{Introduction}

Personalization and policy targeting, the idea that treatment assignment rules should be customized to each individual's characteristics, have gained wide recognition in social and medical sciences. Yet, practice of complete personalization is rare. For example, as noted by \cite{manski2022patient}, President's Council of Advisors on Science and Technology (\citeyear{PCAST}) refers ``personalized medicine'' to:

\emph{``...the tailoring of medical treatment to the specific characteristics of each patient. In an operational sense, however, personalized medicine does not literally mean the creation of drugs or medical devices that are unique to a patient. Rather, it involves the ability to classify individuals into subpopulations that are uniquely or disproportionately susceptible to a particular disease or responsive to a specific treatment.''}

%Indeed, when it comes to the design of personalized policies, decision makers often only form their allocation rules based on a few selected covariates (even though a richer set of observables are present in the data),  due to a variety of fairness, legal, operational or simply logistic and interpretability  concerns. %, certain sensitive characteristics, e.g., gender and race, may not be conditioned on at the policymaking stage, even though these sensitive covariates are observed in the training data. Some individual characteristics are also expensive to collect. As a result, these characteristics may not be observed at the decision making stage even though they are collected in the experiment and are shown to be important factors determining treatment heterogeneity. Even in the absence of legal, fairness or cost concerns, policymakers across different fields often still prefer simple, interpretable decision rules, e.g., threshold rules that evaluate treatment eligibility based on a single index that summaries and coarsens individual characteristics. 
%In this paper, we are particularly interested in the consequences of such practice on the forms of the optimal personalized policies, as well as the corresponding statistical techniques needed to estimate them from finite data. To be more concrete, imagine a decision maker, who wishes to design treatment allocation rules that vary with individual covariates, has collected an administrative or experimental dataset containing a rich array of individual observable characteristics $X\in\mathcal{X}$. However, their actual assignment policies
%can depend only on a subset of $X$, denoted as $W\in\mathcal{W}$, not as rich as $X$. We call these  policies that depend on $W$ only as a class of $W$-individualized rules, a mapping from the support of $W$ to the unit interval indicating treatment fractions. The starting point of our analyses is the simple observation that, although treatment policies can only condition on $W$, treatment effect heterogeneity can still vary at the more refined level of $X$. To what extent should a decision maker be concerned about the potential different patterns of treatment heterogeneity at the levels of $X$ and $W$, respectively?  To answer this question, this paper studies the problem of identifying and estimating an optimal $W$-individualized policy for a regret-averse decision maker, a notion axiomatized by \cite{hayashi2008regret,stoye2011axioms}
%and advocated by \cite*{kitagawa2022treatment}.

Indeed, even though a rich set of observables $X\in\mathcal{X}$ are present in the data, decision makers often only form their allocation rules based on a few selected covariates $W\in\mathcal{W}$, a subset of and not as rich as $X$. The stylized approach (e.g., \citealt{manski2004statistical, kitagawa2018should,AW20,MT17}) maximizes an additive welfare, and the optimal rule only depends on the sign of the average treatment effect conditional on $W$, i.e., CATE($W$). This implies that anyone sharing the same $W$ value receives the same treatment, even though we can learn the finer heterogeneity through CATE($X$). Suppose  CATE($W$) is positive for some $W$, but  analyses via CATE($X$) reveal a considerable proportion of units sharing the same $W$ with negative CATE($X$). Is it still reasonable to  treat everyone for that $W$? What decision criterion is able to reflect heterogeneity of CATE($X$) beyond $W$?   In this paper,  we argue that, even though decision makers can only condition on $W$, it may not make sense to only focus on CATE($W$).  Instead, we present an optimal $W$-individualized rule under the regret-averse criterion that accounts for the heterogeneity over CATE($X$).  
%we study the problem of identifying and estimating an optimal $W$-individualized policy for a regret-averse decision maker, a notion axiomatized by %\cite{hayashi2008regret,stoye2011axioms}
%and advocated by \cite*%%{kitagawa2022treatment}.

In what follows, denote by $Reg(x,\delta)$ the welfare regret of group $X=x$ when it is applied with  a $W$-individualized rule $\delta$, a mapping from $\mathcal{W}$ to the unit interval indicating treatment fractions. The notion of $Reg(x,\delta)$ measures the welfare gap of $\delta$ compared to the best achievable level of welfare for group $X=x$ if individualized allocation based on $X$ were possible. We consider a planner who aggregates regrets among all
subpopulation groups via the average nonlinear regret $L(\delta):=\mathbb{E}\left[Reg^{\alpha}(X,\delta)\right]$, where $\alpha\geq1$ and $\mathbb{E}[\cdot]$ denotes expectation with respect to the marginal distribution of $X$.\footnote{The notion of  nonlinear regret is axiomatized by \cite{hayashi2008regret,stoye2011axioms}
and advocated by \cite*{kitagawa2022treatment}.} %Following the framework of statistical decision theory of \cite{Wald50},  $L(\delta)$ is our loss function. 
A population optimal $W$-individualized rule minimizes $L(\delta)$ among all $W$-individualized rules. The case of $\alpha>1$ is what we call regret aversion. When  $\alpha=1$, the minimization of average welfare regret is the same as maximization of average welfare at a population level,  corresponding to the preceding stylized approach.

Our regret-averse loss function $L(\delta)$ is motivated by one of the fundamental goals of sustainable development policy, namely, to ``reduce the inequalities (\ldots) that (\ldots) undermine the potential of individuals.''\footnote{United Nations Sustainable Development Group, \url{https://unsdg.un.org/2030-agenda/universal-values/leave-no-one-behind}.} In this context, using regret to guide policy targeting is both natural and desirable, as it quantifies how far each group is from achieving its full welfare potential. Furthermore, due to the coarsened nature of the $W$-individualized rule, units sharing the same value of $W$ but with different values of $X$ may display opposite responses to the same treatment. As a result, a $W$-individualized rule that benefits one subgroup  can simultaneously  hurt severely some other group that shares the same $W$ value. Before ranking all decision rules,  a decision maker must take a stance on how they wish to resolve these potential  regret inequalities induced by different $W$-individualized rules. We argue that our regret-averse loss function reflects the preference of a decision maker who is averse to such regret inequalities in the population.  %We show that 
%the regret aversion coefficient $\alpha$ can be interpreted as a measure of regret inequality aversion. 
When $\alpha>1$, the policymaker penalizes rules that lead to greater \emph{regret inequality}, measured by the seminal Atkinson's inequality index \citep{atkinson1970measurement} applied to the distribution of regret in the population. The case of $\alpha = 1$ corresponds to a policymaker who displays no aversion to regret inequality and evaluates rules solely based on their mean regret.

We show that the form of the optimal $W$-individualized rule differs drastically depending on whether the decision maker is regret averse or not.
If $\alpha=1$, the population optimal $W$-individualized rule is a singleton rule (i.e., $\delta(W) = 1$ or $0$) that only depends on the sign of  the CATE($W$), even if the refined covariate $X$ is available from the training data. 
In sharp contrast, whenever $\alpha>1$,  we find that the optimal $W$-individualized rule is fractional in general, and it depends on the  CATE($X$) as well as the dependence between $X$ and $W$.
Consider an artificial example of two groups $X=x_1$ and $X=x_2$ with equal proportion in the population, sharing the same value of $W$ but with opposite treatment responses. 
Any singleton rule will benefit one group but leave the other group with large regret. A regret averse planner will not prefer such corner solution due to the strict convexity of the objective function.\footnote{We stress that the mechanism for fractional rules discussed here is different from that of \cite{kitagawa2022treatment}, in which fractional rules arise due to sampling uncertainty. In our study, fractional rules show up even at a population level without any sampling uncertainty. When a linear welfare is partially identified, minimax regret optimal rules are fractional even with true knowledge of the identified set \citep{Manski2000,manski2005social,manski2007identification, Manski2007}. Without true knowledge of the identified set, finite sample minimax regret optimal rules may also be fractional \citep*{ stoye2012minimax,yata2021,manski2022identification,montielolea2023decision, kitagawa2023treatment}. Fractional rules also show up with  nonlinear welfare in a point-identified setting \citep{manski2007admissible,manski20092009}, when the decision maker targets a functional of the outcome distribution that is not quasi-convex \citep*{kock2022functional,kock2023treatment}, or when agents response with strategic behavior \citep{munro2020learning}. Our mechanisms here are also distinct from those approaches.}

%Having identified the population optimal $W$-individualized rule, we next aim to efficiently learn the optimal $W$-individualized rule from training data. 
Focusing on $\alpha=2$ as a special case for analytical tractability and computational simplicity, we  propose an empirical squared regret minimization approach with debiasing and cross fitting to efficiently learn the optimal $W$-individualized rule from training data. Our procedure can accommodate a wide range of black-box machine learning methods for estimating first-step nuisance parameters. We study  the statistical property of our proposal, and make the following contributions.   First, we derive a %automatic 
debiased form %(c.f., \citealt*{chernozhukov2022automatic}) 
for our  loss function $L(\delta)$, in which nuisance parameters enter non-linearly (due to $\alpha>1$) and non-smoothly (due to an indicator function entering the welfare regret). %In spite of the presence of an indicator function, 
That said, our $L(\delta)$ is still continuously differentiable (though not twice differentiable) when $\alpha=2$. 
This implies that the pathwise derivative formula developed by \cite{newey1994asymptotic}  can still be applied to find the bias correction term.
Second, viewing our debiased criterion as a weighted least squares problem, we develop a new asymptotic upper bound for the excess risk of our proposal. 
In the case of a correctly specified linear sieve policy class, our procedure achieves a fast convergence rate of  $O(1/n)$ (in terms of  excess risk) and is in fact asymptotically efficient. 
These new results rely on the refined maximal inequality developed by \cite{KOHLER20001} applied to  our oracle debiased squared regret criterion (which is different from the techniques based on \citealt{vapnik1971uniform,vapnik1974theory}), as well as an optimality condition due to the least squares structure to control for the remaining estimation error (which also differs from the approach exploited in \citealt{AW20}). 
Third, we also derive a minimax lower bound for the excess risk of our procedure in the style of \cite{stone1982optimal},  which is useful for future optimality studies of our procedure  in case of a genuinely nonparametric optimal $W$-individualized rule.

In terms of computation, our squared regret approach is attractive due to the weighted least squares structure of the objective function. And it will be straightforward to accommodate policy classes with convex constraints,  compared to the mean regret approach (which often relies on maximum score type optimization).   

%For future research, it would also be interesting to study to what extent our debised approach is strictly needed for some of our theoretic results to go through, in light of the ``low bias'' results of certain plug-in approaches
 %in the semiparametric estimation literature (c.f.,  \citealt*{ai2003efficient,chen2003estimation,hirano2003efficient}).

We illustrate the value of our approach using two real datasets. For the National JTPA Study that measured the benefit and cost of employment and training programs, %for workers in the U.S.,
we consider a policy maker who designs treatment policies based on  pre-program years of education and earnings only, even though a wider range of characteristics like gender, race and marital status are available in the data. For the International Stroke Trial data that assessed
the effect of aspirin treatment for patients with acute ischemic stroke, we considered a hypothetical scenario  in which a doctor determines whether a patient should be treated with aspirin based on their age only, even though the training data contains many covariates of the patients, including their  demographic and medical history. 
In both datasets, the estimated optimal policy fractions with our squared regret approach reveal considerable treatment effect heterogeneity  for some population subgroups, which can lead to significant regret inequality  should a singleton policy be applied instead. 
These exercises suggest that our squared regret approach reveals  additional important information that may not be assessed with the mean regret paradigm alone. 

%The Atkinson inequality measure baked in our loss function belongs to a class of  inequality indices that
%satisfies the Pigou-Dalton principle of transfers, scale invariance, and decomposability,  are well studied in the inequality literature (see, \citealt{cowell2011measuring,cowell2000measurement,cowell1981additivity,shorrocks1984inequality} and references therein), and are different from the more popular Gini-inequality index (see e.g., \citealt{KT21,terschuur2024locally}). Our loss function is grounded in the axiomatic work of \cite{hayashi2008regret,stoye2011axioms} that studies the choice
%of a regret-averse planner, while \cite{yaari1987dual,yaari1988controversial} embeds Gini-inequality in the dual
%theory of a risk averse planner with rank-dependent utility. See Section \ref{sec:inequalty.aversion} for additional discussions.

The treatment choice literature has become an area of active research since the pioneering works of \cite{Manski2000, manski2002treatment,manski2004statistical} and \cite{Dehejia2005}. When the policy maker cannot differentiate individuals based on observable characteristics, finite and asymptotic results are developed by  \cite{schlag2006eleven}, \cite{stoye2009minimax},  \cite{HiranoPorter2009}, \cite{tetenov2012statistical}, \cite{masten2023minimax}, and \cite{chen2024note}.  \cite{manski2014quantile} and \cite*{guggenberger2024minimax}  in point-identified situations, and by  \cite{Manski2000,manski2005social,manski2007identification, Manski2007, manski20092009},  \cite{stoye2012minimax}, \cite{christensen2020}, \cite{ishihara2021}, \cite{yata2021}, \cite{manski2022identification}, \cite{ishihara2023bandwidth} and \cite{montielolea2023decision} in partially-identified settings. When the policy maker is able to condition on individual characteristics, studies on personalization and policy targeting include \cite{manski2004statistical}, \cite{BhattacharyaDupas2012}, \cite{kitagawa2018should, KT21}, \cite{MT17}, \cite{AW20}, \cite{sun2021empirical}, \cite{adjaho2022externally}, \cite{han2023optimal}, \cite{cui2023individualized},
\cite{terschuur2024locally}, \cite{viviano2024policy} and \cite{viviano2024fair}, among others, for analyses in different settings. 

Our squared regret criterion coincides with a quadratic surrogate criterion for a cost-sensitive classification problem to predict the sign of CATE($X$) with cost being squared CATE($X$). See \cite{Zhang_2004}, \cite*{Bartlett_et_al_2006}, and references therein for general analyses of surrogate risk minimization for classification. Note, however, that our approach fundamentally differs from classification with the quadratic surrogate since our approach takes the squared regret as the ultimate objective function to minimize rather than a surrogate for the binary classification loss. As a result, our analysis can allow constrained $W$-individualized rules without raising the inconsistency issue of the constrained classification studied in \cite*{KST21}.

The rest of the paper is organized as follows: Section \ref{sec:setup} sets the stage, motivates our regret-averse loss function via inequality aversion and  studies the population optimal $W$-individualized rule. Section \ref{sec:proposal} presents our main proposal. Section \ref{sec:stat} develops the statistical performance guarantee. Empirical applications are in Section \ref{sec:emp.app}. Additional proofs, lemmas and technical results  are reserved in the Appendix and Online Supplement. 


%\section{Empirical Illustration}

